/* Purview Gurukul — Purview SOPs: ABW Medical client playbook.
   Built from internal ABW documents: the AR Notes Reference Guide, the
   Consolidated Client Operations Manual (six practices, updates to Sept 2026),
   and the ABW SOPs for athenaOne (the Workflow SOP, the Consolidated SOP,
   How to run Reports and the Unpostables Workflow), with ABW’s October 2026 answers
   to Purview’s questions. Screenshots are left out.
   Personal names, contact details, account numbers and commercial terms are
   deliberately left out; the COM itself remains the source of truth for those. */
"use strict";
const SOP_CLIENTS=[
{id:"abw",name:"ABW Medical",short:"ABW",updated:"COM updates to 29 September 2026, ABW athena SOPs 2026, ABW answers October 2026",
 truth:"The approved Client Operations Manuals (COMs), the ABW SOPs and the SAAG guidelines (the kick-code file) are the source of truth; rules change on the weekly client call.",
 blurb:"Purview staff work as ABW’s AR and billing team for six US practices, mostly on athenahealth through Privia. This playbook covers how ABW works, the standard note, the client-by-client rules, the workflows that trip people up, and the athenaOne SOPs: Hold and MGR Hold, kick codes, Zero Pay, Unpostables and patient payments.",
 modules:[
{id:"abw-a",short:"ABW basics",title:"How ABW works",blurb:"Who the client is, the work types, the systems, and where decisions get parked.",lessons:[
 {id:"abw-who",title:"ABW Medical and the Client Operations Manual",
  keywords:["abw","who is abw","abw medical","client operations manual","com","passive approval","weekly call","clarification log","pending log","car report","client assistance","account manager","escalation abw"],
  line:"ABW Medical is a US revenue cycle company. Purview staff work as its AR and billing team for six practices, and every practice has a Client Operations Manual (COM) that says what we do, what we never do, and what needs the practice’s decision.",
  body:[
   "**The setup.** ABW contracts with physician practices, most of them Privia care centres running athenahealth. ABW assigns an account manager per client, with a Revenue Cycle Solutions Manager as first escalation and a Vice President of Delivery as second. Each client also has a shared team mailbox. Names and numbers live in the COM contact sheet, not here.",
   "**The COM.** Every client has a Client Operations Manual with the same shape: a version history, ABW and client contacts, client information, the scope of work, a questionnaire the client answered, and dated protocol updates. The COM is sent to the client and counts as approved if the client does not object: the manuals call this **passive approval**. Updates arrive on the weekly client call or by email and are added with their date. The consolidated COM itself warns that its client rules and dates have not been reconciled or independently updated, so read every dated entry in the practice’s section and confirm the current guidance before acting.",
   "**Where undecided things go.** When a claim cannot be resolved by ABW effort it is escalated for practice assistance. Two logs do that work: the **clarification log** (also called the pending log), emailed daily with the claims that need a practice decision and the reason, and the **CAR**, the client assistance report where accounts are parked until the practice answers. A good entry states the exact decision needed, never just ‘pending client’.",
   "**What this playbook is.** A training version of the ABW documents: the AR Notes Reference Guide, the consolidated COM, and the ABW SOPs for athenaOne (navigation, Hold and MGR Hold, kick codes, Zero Pay, Unpostables, patient AR and reports). It teaches the rules; it does not replace the COMs, the SOPs, payer rules, coding guidance or required approvals. If a rule here disagrees with the latest COM or SOP, the COM or SOP wins."
  ],
  terms:[["Client Operations Manual (COM)","The per-client document holding scope, questionnaire answers and dated protocols; approved by passive approval."],["Passive approval","The COM counts as signed off if the client sends no feedback after it is emailed."],["Clarification log / pending log","Daily email to the practice listing claims that need its decision, each with a reason."],["CAR (client assistance report)","Where accounts wait for a practice decision; entries must name the decision needed."],["Weekly call","The standing client call where protocol updates are agreed; they are added to the COM with the date."],["Account manager","ABW’s owner of the client relationship and first stop for questions; escalation runs to the RCM Solutions Manager, then the VP of Delivery."]],
  tips:["Before you act on any client rule, read that client’s dated updates and confirm the current guidance; several rules changed between March and September 2026.","Never put a client’s contact details, a patient’s account number or a login in a training note or a chat. Minimum necessary applies to us too."],
  remember:"Six practices, one manual each, passive approval, weekly updates with dates. If ABW effort cannot resolve it, it goes to the clarification log or the CAR with the exact decision needed.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, version histories and contact sections (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-scope",title:"The ABW work types and their boundaries",
  keywords:["abw work types","hold bucket","hold buckets","insurance ar follow up","patient ar","front end coding","coding denial","charge entry","demographics","auth and vob","scope of work","what does abw do","abw tasks"],
  line:"ABW sells a short list of work types. Each one has a boundary the scopes of work spell out: add modifiers but never pick codes, work only from the start date, escalate what we cannot resolve, and never act as a collections agency.",
  body:[
   "**Hold buckets and Manager Hold.** Reviewing and resolving every claim athena parks in Hold, escalating to the practice what ABW effort cannot fix. Manager Hold is a narrower, named list of claim-change events; for one client it excludes anything about credentialing, web access, overpayments and virtual credit cards.",
   "**Zero Pay.** Working the Zero Pay tracker: charges the payer paid nothing on, refiled or appealed where they can still be collected. The ABW SOP adds how: review the claims athena adjusted to $0 for primary payers and check each closure or adjustment.",
   "**Insurance AR follow-up.** Chasing outstanding insurance balances older than the client’s preference, usually 60 days from the date of service, and working any issue found under the client’s protocols. It is in the Atwood, Restora and Space Coast scopes.",
   "**Patient AR.** Reviewing patient balances for legitimacy, calling patients with real balances to take payment or set up a plan, adjusting illegitimate balances per practice protocol, requesting missing information, and leaving voicemails when unreachable. ABW works as an extension of the practice team. It does not do hard collections and is not a collections agency.",
   "**Coding.** Front-end coding picks CPT and diagnosis codes from provider notes for office visits and procedures and either reports them or enters them in athena; it needs clinical access and a charge-entry task in scope to send the claims. Coding-denial work corrects CPT and diagnosis codes on held claims from the documentation available.",
   "**The rest.** Unpostables (athena’s dashboard of unmatched EOBs and pay cards), charge entry for inpatient services, demographics entry from the practice’s billing sheet (no eligibility check), and prior authorisation plus benefits verification for the appointment types the client lists."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Work type","We do","We never do"],rows:[["Hold / Manager Hold","Resolve what we can; escalate the rest with a reason","Sit on a claim without an escalation entry"],["Zero Pay","Refile or appeal collectable zero-paid charges","Adjust without the client’s rule allowing it"],["Insurance AR","Follow up balances past the client’s age threshold","Promise a payer timeframe we did not confirm"],["Patient AR","Call, take payment, set plans, request missing info, voicemail","Hard collections; discounts the client did not approve"],["Coding","Code from provider notes with clinical access","Code visits dated before the coding start date"]]},
  terms:[["Hold bucket","athena’s queue of claims stopped by a rule or a denial, each with a kick reason."],["Manager Hold (MGRHOLD)","A status for claims that need a manager’s review and decision before they can be processed; the MGRHOLD kick code sends a claim there (rarely, when requested) with a note saying why. Also used to park payer-defect claims."],["Zero pay","A charge processed and closed at $0; worked from the Zero Pay tracker."],["Unpostables","athena’s list of payments and EOBs it could not match to a claim."],["Scope of work (SOW)","The list of work types, volumes and start dates ABW is contracted for; not in this playbook."],["Clinical access","athena clinical rights that coders need to read provider notes."]],
  tips:["If a held claim needs a code added or changed and the client has no coding resource in scope, it is a clarification-log item, not a quick fix.","‘Over 60 days from DOS’ is a client preference, not a law. Check the client card before you decide what counts as follow-up AR."],
  remember:"Modifiers yes, codes no. Start date yes, earlier no. Escalate with a reason. Patient AR is the practice’s voice, never a collections agency.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, scope of work sections (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-tools",title:"athena, Privia, ECHO and the payer portals",
  keywords:["athena","athenahealth","privia","echo","privia care center","athena hold","kick reason","scrub","local rule","billing tab","provider review box","sticky note","where to check status","no eob in athena","clearinghouse abw"],
  line:"Five of the six ABW practices run athenahealth under Privia. Knowing what each system holds decides where you look first, and the manuals are clear: athena, then the payer portal, then ECHO, before you write ‘no status available’.",
  body:[
   "**athena (athenahealth).** The practice management system for claims, payments and holds. Claims stop in Hold buckets with a kick reason. Scrub rules can reject a claim internally before it ever reaches the payer; athena’s local rules suggest modifiers (the QW lab modifier, for instance) and most clients let ABW follow those suggestions. The Billing tab has a Provider Review box, there is a sticky note on the patient for front-desk instructions, and the Unpostables dashboard lists money athena could not place.",
   "**Privia.** A management services organisation. Each practice is a Privia care centre with a Customer Success Manager, and Privia’s own RCM team handles some things centrally: refunds for at least one client, payer-wide defect clean-ups, and the patient line that sets up payment plans. When Privia sends a directive, it overrides the usual claim-by-claim routine.",
   "**ECHO.** A payment and remittance portal. When athena shows no clear status or EOB, check the payer portal and ECHO before concluding nothing is available; retrieve, save and upload the EOB and record the batch or reference for posting.",
   "**Payer portals and the clearinghouse.** Status, EOBs and rejection reasons. A clearinghouse rejection means the payer never received the claim, which is why the note must say ‘payer acceptance is not confirmed’ and the timely filing clock keeps running.",
   "**The order of checking.** athena first, then the payer portal, then ECHO, then a call. Every note records which source was checked and on what date."
  ],
  terms:[["athena local rule","A practice-level rule that suggests or demands a change, such as adding a modifier, before the claim can drop."],["Scrub failure","athena stopping a claim internally; the payer has not seen it, so the filing clock keeps running."],["Kick reason (kick code)","The code chosen with a claim action that records why and sends the claim to its next queue, such as DRPBILLING or INFORM; athena’s own holds carry one too, such as ‘Rendering’ or ‘Clinical Order Review’."],["Rule 7000 (Clinical Order Review)","An athena hold that most clients allow ABW to override; one client wants it left and logged."],["ECHO","Payment and remittance portal checked when athena has no EOB."],["Privia Customer Success Manager","Privia’s contact for a care centre; listed in each COM."]],
  tips:["A ‘no status’ note that names only athena is incomplete. Portal and ECHO are part of the check.","If a Privia email says do not resubmit, that beats everything in your queue while it is active. Leave the claims as the directive says (in the 2026 CareFirst case athena moved them to Manager Hold itself) and note the directive."],
  remember:"athena → payer portal → ECHO → call. Record the source and the date every time.",
  source:"ABW AR Notes Reference Guide section 6 and COM protocol sections (internal)."}
]},
{id:"abw-b",short:"ABW notes",title:"The ABW standard note",blurb:"Six things every note has, copy-ready templates for each scenario, and the quality check before you save.",lessons:[
 {id:"abw-note-structure",title:"The standard note: six things every note has",
  keywords:["abw note","note format","note structure","how to write a note","call note","non-call note","account note","what goes in a note","standard note","documentation standard abw"],
  line:"Every ABW account note has the same six parts: who the account is, what source you checked, the verified status in the payer’s words, what you actually did, what happens next and by when, and for calls the date, time, rep and reference number.",
  body:[
   "**1. Identify the account** using the client-approved identifiers: account or patient ID, date of service, payer, and claim number if there is one.",
   "**2. Record the source checked:** athena, the payer portal, the clearinghouse, the EOB or ERA, ECHO where applicable, or a phone call. Include the date you checked.",
   "**3. State the verified status** and the exact denial or reason wording. Never describe a pending claim as approved, and never paraphrase a denial reason into something softer.",
   "**4. Record what was actually done:** submitted, corrected, appealed, uploaded, routed, or checked. A planned action is written as a next step, not as something done.",
   "**5. State the next action, the owner and the follow-up date** or the payer’s timeframe. ‘Follow up by 10/24’ beats ‘will follow up’.",
   "**6. For calls, add the date and time, the representative’s name or ID if given, and the payer reference number.** Without the reference number the call did not happen as far as the next reviewer is concerned.",
   "**Two kinds of note.** A **non-call note** records a review done in systems. A **call note** records a conversation. The templates come in pairs, one of each, for every scenario. Document only actions actually completed and facts confirmed by the payer, portal, EOB/ERA or client; replace every bracketed field with verified details and delete anything unverified."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Weak note","ABW note"],rows:[["Called payer, claim pending, will follow up","Called [PAYER] on 10/14 at 11:05 regarding DOS 08/02, claim #[number]. Spoke with [rep]. Ref #[number]. Payer confirmed claim received 09/30, in process. Payer advised 30-day TAT. Next step: recheck portal by 10/30."],["Denied, appealing","EOB shows denial code [code] and reason [exact wording]. Appeal submitted 10/14 via portal with [documents], confirmation #[number]. Appeal deadline per payer: 11/28. Follow up 10/28."],["Pending client","Client input required: approve adjustment of $[amount] for [reason] or confirm patient billing. Account remains pending until direction is received. Follow up 10/21."]]},
  terms:[["Non-call note","A note recording a system review: what was checked, found and done."],["Call note","A note recording a payer, patient or practice call: date, time, rep, reference, what was confirmed."],["Verified status","The status as the payer, portal, EOB/ERA or client stated it, in their words."],["Reference number","The payer’s call reference; the proof the call happened."],["Next step","Action, owner and date, written so another person can act on it."]],
  tips:["Write the status in the payer’s words, then your action, then the next step. That order survives any reviewer.","If a bracket is still in your note, you are not finished."],
  remember:"Account, source, verified status, action done, next step with owner and date, and for calls the rep and reference number. Facts only; placeholders replaced.",
  source:"ABW AR Notes Reference Guide sections 1 and 2 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-note-status",title:"Templates: status, payment and ‘cannot reach’",
  keywords:["no claim on file note","claim in process note","approved to pay note","paid but not posted","payment not posted note","no eob note","voicemail note","unable to reach note","claim status note template","copy ready note","note template"],
  line:"Copy-ready pairs for the everyday cases: nothing on file, in process, approved, paid but not posted, no EOB anywhere, and the call that did not connect. Replace the brackets, delete what you did not verify.",
  body:[
   "**Standard non-call review.** Reviewed account for DOS [MM/DD/YYYY] under [PAYER]. Checked [athena/payer portal/clearinghouse/EOB/ERA] on [date]. Current status: [verified status]. [Denial/reason, if any]. Action taken: [completed action]. Next step: [action/owner] by [date].",
   "**Standard call.** Called [PAYER] on [date] at [time] regarding DOS [date], claim #[number]. Spoke with [rep name/ID]. Reference #[number]. Payer confirmed [exact status/reason]. Action taken: [action]. Payer advised [timeframe/requirement, if confirmed]. Next step: [action] by [date].",
   "**No claim number available.** Reviewed [system/portal] for DOS [date] and payer [name]; claim number/status not available. Checked [clearinghouse/other source] with result [result]. Next step: [verify submission details/obtain acceptance proof/contact payer/request practice support] by [date].",
   "**No claim on file.** Non-call: Reviewed [payer] portal and [clearinghouse] for DOS [date]. No claim on file as of [date]. Verified member ID, payer, DOS and provider/submission details. Next step: [check rejection/confirm submission proof/resubmit/contact payer] by [date]. Call: rep [name/ID] confirmed no claim on file for DOS [date], reference #[number], rep advised [submission method, if confirmed]; action [taken]; follow up by [date].",
   "**Received, in process, pending.** Non-call: Portal shows claim received/in process for DOS [date], claim #[number], last updated [date]. No final determination yet. Recheck on [date or payer timeframe]. Call: rep confirmed claim #[number] is [received/in process/pending], reference #[number], payer advised [timeframe, if confirmed]; follow up on [date].",
   "**Approved or processed to pay.** Non-call: EOB/portal shows claim processed with payment of [$] and patient responsibility of [$]. Payment status [issued/pending EFT/check/paid] per [source]. Next step: [verify deposit/post payment/review ERA] and address the remaining balance per EOB and client policy. Call: rep confirmed payment of [$] [approved/issued] on [date] by [EFT/check details], reference #[number]; next step reconcile against EOB/ERA and post.",
   "**Paid but not posted.** Non-call: EOB/ERA indicates payment of [$] on [date] not reflected in the account. EOB/ERA located at [location]; batch #[number]. Routed for posting/reconciliation. Follow up if not posted by [date]. The ABW posting note reads: ‘Payment received; EOB uploaded. Batch #____. Payment needs to be posted.’ Use it only when the payment and EOB really are in hand.",
   "**No EOB or unclear portal status.** Non-call: No EOB or definitive status available in [system] as of [date]. Checked [portal/clearinghouse]. Next step: check payer portal and ECHO; retrieve, save and upload the EOB and record batch/reference details. Call: called [payer] because no EOB/status was available; rep confirmed [status/no record]; reference #[number]; next step [request EOB/recheck/contact clearinghouse/route for posting] by [date].",
   "**Voicemail or unable to reach.** Attempted contact with [payer/patient/practice] on [date] at [time]; no live response. [Voicemail left, only if actually done and permitted]. Retry on [date] or use the approved alternate method. A failed call is still a note: ‘call attempt unsuccessful; no confirmation obtained; retry and document outcome.’"
  ],
  tips:["‘Payment needs to be posted’ is a handoff to the posting team. Give them the batch number or they cannot act.","A recheck date on an in-process claim should match the payer’s stated turnaround, not a habit."],
  remember:"Status notes carry the source, the date, the payer’s exact words and a dated next step. A call that did not connect is still documented.",
  source:"ABW AR Notes Reference Guide sections 2 and 3 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-note-denials",title:"Templates: denials, appeals and handoffs",
  keywords:["denial note template","appeal note","corrected claim note","reprocessing note","appeal upheld note","appeal overturned","clearinghouse rejection note","duplicate denial note","timely filing note","eligibility denial note","authorization denial note","medical records note","coding denial note","medical necessity note","cob note","underpayment note","recoupment note","client clarification note","adjustment approval note","work completed note"],
  line:"One pair of templates per denial family, and six handoff notes for when the next move belongs to the practice, coding, the authorisation team or the patient. The shape never changes: exact reason, what was validated, what was submitted with its confirmation number, and a dated follow-up.",
  body:[
   "**Denied, payer reprocessing needed.** EOB shows denial code [code], reason [exact wording]. Review identified [verified payer-processing issue]. [Reprocessing/reconsideration] requested on [date], confirmation #[number]. Follow up by [date]. On a call: rep confirmed denial for [reason]; requested reprocessing based on [verified facts]; reference #[number]; payer advised [next step/timeframe].",
   "**Denied, corrected claim required.** Claim denied for [reason/code]. Required correction: [specific validated correction]. Corrected claim submitted on [date] with [correction], trace/confirmation #[number]. Monitor adjudication and confirm receipt. Always record the correction made, the submission date, the trace number and the follow-up date.",
   "**Denied, appeal required / appeal pending / upheld / overturned.** Appeal [submitted/not yet submitted] on [date] via [method] with [documents], confirmation #[number], appeal deadline if verified [date], follow up [date]. Pending: tracking #[number], portal shows [pending/received] as of [date]. Upheld: determination dated [date] upheld denial for [reason]; next step [second-level appeal/client review/adjustment request] per payer rules and client instructions; validate patient responsibility before billing. Overturned: claim to be reprocessed; monitor for final adjudication and verify EOB/payment before closing.",
   "**Clearinghouse rejection.** Clearinghouse rejected claim for [exact message/code] on [date]; payer acceptance is not confirmed. Corrected [field] and resubmitted on [date], trace #[number]. Confirm acceptance.",
   "**Duplicate.** Compared DOS, claim number, amount and related claim #[number]. Related claim status: [verified]. Next step: [confirm prior adjudication/request reprocessing/appeal with proof].",
   "**Timely filing.** Reviewed original submission/acceptance proof; available proof: [details]. Next step: submit reconsideration/appeal with proof by [verified deadline], or request support if proof is unavailable.",
   "**Eligibility, authorisation, records, coding, medical necessity, COB, out-of-network, bundled.** Each follows the same pattern: EOB shows [exact reason]; what you checked and found; the next step by the right owner. Authorisation: checked available auth records [result]; next step verify auth, request retro-auth if permitted, appeal, or route to the practice. Records: payer requests [specific records], deadline [date], submitted on [date] via [method], confirmation #[number]. Coding: routed to coding/provider review; submit a correction or appeal only after validation. Out-of-network or non-covered: validate contract and benefit terms and get client direction on appeal, adjustment or patient responsibility; **do not bill the patient until validated.**",
   "**Underpayment and recoupment.** Underpayment: compared EOB payment/allowed with the contract or fee schedule; variance $[amount] for [reason]; next step payment dispute or contract review. Recoupment: EOB/ERA indicates recoupment of $[amount] for [claim/reason]; validate amount and reason, link related claims, route for finance or client review if unclear.",
   "**Handoff notes.** Client clarification: issue, findings, actions completed, and the exact decision the practice must make; the account stays pending until direction is received. Adjustment approval: reason, balance, approval requested on [date] for [type/amount/reason]. Coding review: exact reason, the specific question, routed to [team] on [date] with supporting details. Authorisation team: DOS, CPT, provider, what records show, routed on [date]. Patient contact: issue, attempt on [date] via [approved method], outcome, next step per client process. Work completed: reviewed [source], completed [action], verified status, why no further AR action is pending, and what would reopen it."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Denial family","What the note must prove"],rows:[["Eligibility / member not eligible","Member ID, payer, coverage dates, other active coverage checked"],["Missing or invalid information","The exact missing field, validated against a source before correcting"],["Authorisation / referral","Auth number, CPT, provider, DOS, referral and payer requirement"],["Medical necessity / policy","Records, coding review, payer policy, appeal criteria"],["CPT / ICD-10 / modifier","Routed to coding or provider review; codes never changed without support"],["Duplicate","Related claim status and any prior payment"],["Timely filing","Original acceptance proof and the verified appeal deadline"],["Non-covered / benefit exclusion","EOB and plan benefit; client direction before adjustment or patient billing"],["Bundled / inclusive","Related services and the payer’s rationale; coding review if needed"],["COB / other insurance","Primary and secondary order and the coverage update needed"],["Credentialing / enrolment","Provider enrolment status and effective dates; routed to credentialing or the practice"],["Medical records","Exact documents requested, due date, submission proof and receipt"],["Underpayment","Allowed, paid, contract or fee schedule, variance calculation"],["Patient responsibility","EOB deductible, copay, coinsurance and the client-approved billing workflow"]]},
  tips:["For appeals the note always carries six facts: denial reason, verified deadline, method and date, documents, confirmation number, next follow-up.","‘Routed to coding’ is an action only if the note says to whom, on what date, with what question."],
  remember:"Exact reason, what you validated, what you submitted with its confirmation number, who owns the next step, and the date. Patient billing waits for validation.",
  source:"ABW AR Notes Reference Guide sections 3, 4 and 5 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-note-qa",title:"Workflow reminders and the quality check before saving",
  keywords:["quality check","before saving a note","note qa","abw reminders","adjust denials","do not move to patient responsibility","abbreviations abw","checklist note"],
  line:"Eight workflow reminders and seven questions to ask before you save. Together they stop the two most expensive note mistakes: writing something unverified, and moving a balance to the patient without support.",
  body:[
   "**Workflow reminders.** If athena has no clear status or EOB, check the payer portal and ECHO before concluding no status is available. When an EOB is available, identify whether it shows payment, patient responsibility, denial or contractual adjustment before acting. Use the posting note only when payment and EOB are actually in hand. Adjust denials only when supported by the EOB, client instructions and the required approval. Do not move balances to patient responsibility without validation. For corrected claims record the correction, submission date, trace number and follow-up date. For appeals record the denial reason, verified deadline, method and date, documents, confirmation number and next follow-up. For pending client assistance state the exact decision needed. Use minimum-necessary account details and follow the client’s privacy and documentation policy.",
   "**The quality check.** Is the current status verified and the issue clearly stated? Is the source documented: portal, EOB/ERA, clearinghouse or call? For calls, are date, time, representative if given, and reference number included? Does the note separate completed action from planned action? Is the next step, owner and follow-up date clear? Are any adjustment, patient billing, corrected claim or appeal actions supported by payer guidance and client SOP? Have all placeholders been replaced and unverified statements removed?",
   "**Abbreviations the guide allows.** AR accounts receivable; DOS date of service; EOB explanation of benefits; ERA electronic remittance advice; CARC/RARC claim adjustment and remittance advice reason codes; COB coordination of benefits; CPT/HCPCS procedure codes; ICD-10 diagnosis codes; EFT electronic funds transfer; OON out of network; auth authorisation. Use full words where they help the next reviewer."
  ],
  flow:{steps:["Verified status and issue stated in the payer’s words","Source and date documented","Call details complete: date, time, rep, reference","Completed action separated from planned action","Next step with owner and follow-up date","Adjustment, patient billing, corrected claim or appeal supported by payer guidance and client SOP","No placeholders left, nothing unverified"],to:"Save the note"},
  terms:[["Contractual adjustment","The EOB amount the provider writes off under its contract; never patient responsibility."],["Patient responsibility","Deductible, copay and coinsurance as the EOB shows them; billable only under the client’s rule."],["Minimum necessary","Only the account details the task needs, in the note and in any email."]],
  tips:["Read your note as the person who picks the account up in three weeks. If they would have to call again to learn what you learned, add it.","When a denial is adjusted, the note names the rule that allowed it: the EOB line, the client instruction, or the approval."],
  remember:"Verified, sourced, dated, owner named, supported, no brackets. Patient responsibility only after validation.",
  source:"ABW AR Notes Reference Guide sections 6, 7 and 8 (internal)."}
]},
{id:"abw-c",short:"Client rules",title:"Client rules: bill, adjust or park it",blurb:"The five decision areas every client answers, the answers side by side, and the decision flow for a balance.",lessons:[
 {id:"abw-questionnaire",title:"The five decision areas every client answers",
  keywords:["questionnaire","hold questionnaire","client questionnaire","can we bill the patient","can we adjust","small balance","denial coding corrections","bill patient rules","adjustment rules","credentialing rules abw","vcc","virtual credit card","rule 7000","qw modifier","new to established"],
  line:"Before a client goes live, ABW asks the same eighteen questions in five areas: what coding corrections we may make, when we may bill the patient, what we may adjust, what we may change about providers, and what to do with virtual credit card payments. The client’s answers become the rules you work by.",
  body:[
   "**1. Denial coding corrections (when no coder is assigned).** May we add or remove modifiers on athena’s local-rule suggestions? May we change a new-patient code to established after checking the billing history? May we add or correct the basic modifiers 24, 25, 59, 51, 76 and 77? May we override athena rule 7000, Clinical Order Review? May we add the QW modifier to lab codes when athena suggests it? In every case the fallback is the same: no proper athena suggestion, verify with the practice.",
   "**2. Bill the patient?** If the policy was inactive on the date of service, and does that include Medicaid? If information is needed from the patient (COB, questionnaire, accident details)? If the balance is copay, coinsurance or deductible? For non-covered services and out-of-network providers, will we bring it to the practice case by case? If demographics are invalid or missing (date of birth, name, insurance ID)?",
   "**3. Adjust?** Timely filing exceeded with no proof and the appeal upheld? Small balances under a threshold the client sets? Exact duplicates created by the athena interface or incorrect billing? Primary paid more than the secondary allowed? Specific codes non-covered under the provider fee schedule?",
   "**4. Provider credentialing and enrolment.** May we add a referring provider from Quick View or the chart and drop the claim? If a payer tells us a provider’s PAR or non-PAR status on a call, may we update athena and release the claim?",
   "**5. Deposit confirmation.** When a payer pays by virtual credit card (VCC), do we call to request a check or EFT instead, or may we take the VCC in athena within its time limit?",
   "**Why it matters.** The answer is the instruction. A ‘yes’ or a stated action (‘bill patient’, a $5 limit, ‘use the supervising provider’) tells you what to do; a ‘no’ means do not do it; a blank or ‘place it in CAR’ means park the claim for the practice. The same denial can be adjusted for one client, billed to the patient for a second, and escalated for a third."
  ],
  terms:[["Athena local-rule suggestion","athena proposing a modifier or code change; the condition most clients attach to coding corrections."],["Basic modifiers 24, 25, 59, 51, 76, 77","Modifiers ABW may add or correct when the client allows and athena suggests: unrelated E/M in a global period, separate E/M same day, distinct service, multiple procedures, repeat by same or another physician."],["QW","Modifier for CLIA-waived lab tests."],["Small balance adjustment","Writing off a balance below the client’s threshold instead of chasing it."],["VCC (virtual credit card)","A payer payment by one-time card number, with a processing fee; some clients refuse it."],["PAR / non-PAR","Whether a provider is contracted with the payer; decides network status on the claim."]],
  tips:["Keep the five headings in your head: coding corrections, bill patient, adjust, credentialing, VCC. Any question about a balance belongs under one of them.","A blank answer is not a yes. Park it and ask."],
  remember:"Eighteen questions, five areas. Do what the answer says; a blank or ‘place in CAR’ means park it with the decision needed.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, Hold / Manager Hold questionnaires (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-rules-matrix",title:"The answers, client by client",
  keywords:["client by client","rules matrix","which client allows","small balance atwood","small balance one oak","small balance restora","small balance san marcos","small balance space coast","small balance upstate","medicaid bill patient","can we bill medicaid patient","timely filing adjust","duplicate adjust","primary paid more than secondary","vcc by client","referring provider by client"],
  line:"Six practices, one grid. Read your client’s column before you bill, adjust or override anything; the same question has a different answer in almost every column.",
  body:[
   "**How to read the grid.** Rows are the questionnaire questions in short form. ‘Yes’ means ABW may act. ‘Park’ means put it in the CAR or clarification log with the decision needed. ‘Ask’ means the client left it blank. Upstate’s manual sets patient AR and benefits rules rather than a Hold questionnaire, so most of its column is ‘ask’.",
   "**Three rows people get wrong.** Inactive policy: four clients let us bill the patient, but Atwood wants Medicaid patients switched to self-pay first, One Oak says no for Medicaid, and San Marcos does not accept Medicaid at all. Information needed from the patient: One Oak holds the claim and contacts the practice; Restora bills but calls the patient first. Timely filing with the appeal upheld: adjustable everywhere except One Oak, which wants the practice to review first, and Space Coast, which wants management involved when the cause was a TIN update.",
   "**Thresholds.** Small balance: Atwood none, One Oak $25, Restora $5, San Marcos $5, Space Coast $40, Upstate $25 for patient AR. Below the threshold the balance may be adjusted; at or above it, it is worked."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Question","Atwood","One Oak","Restora","San Marcos","Space Coast","Upstate"],rows:[
   ["Modifiers per athena suggestions","Yes; verify if none","Yes, not on health assessments","Ask","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["New → established patient code","Yes, verify if needed","Yes","Ask","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Basic modifiers 24/25/59/51/76/77","Yes, verify if needed","Yes, not on health assessments","Ask","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Override rule 7000","No: wait and log in CAR","Yes","Ask","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Add QW on labs","Yes","Yes","Ask","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Bill patient: policy inactive","Yes; Medicaid → remove insurance, self-pay, then bill","Yes commercial/Medicare; no Medicaid","Yes, call first","Yes; practice takes no Medicaid","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Bill patient: info needed (COB, questionnaire, accident)","Yes","No: hold and contact practice","Yes, call first","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Bill patient: copay, coinsurance, deductible","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes"],
   ["Non-covered / out of network","Adjust; no approval needed","Park, case by case","Bill patient","Park, case by case","Park, case by case","Ask"],
   ["Bill patient: invalid demographics","Yes","No: hold and contact practice","Yes","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Adjust: TFL exceeded, appeal upheld","Yes","No: practice reviews first","Yes","Yes","Yes; TIN-related needs management","Ask"],
   ["Small balance threshold","None","$25","$5","Under $5","$40","$25 (patient AR)"],
   ["Adjust exact duplicates","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Adjust when primary paid more than secondary allowed","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Adjust specific non-covered codes","Park in CAR","Hold and ask","Park in CAR; depression screening may be adjusted","No; track them","Depends on charge","Ask"],
   ["Add referring provider from chart","Use the supervising provider","Yes","Park in CAR","Verify with practice","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Update PAR/non-PAR from a call","Yes, document the findings","Yes","Park in CAR","Yes","Yes","Ask"],
   ["Virtual credit card","Not accepted; call for other payment","Call for check or EFT","Ask","EFT","Call for check or EFT","Ask"]
  ]},
  tips:["When a client answer says ‘verify if needed’, that means athena gave no suggestion. With a suggestion you act; without one you ask.","Save the grid as your first stop for any Hold claim. Column first, then row."],
  remember:"Same question, different column, different answer. Check the column before the claim.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, questionnaires for all six practices (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-decide",title:"A balance is sitting there: bill, adjust, or park it?",
  keywords:["what to do with a balance","should i adjust","should i bill the patient","adjust or bill","decision flow","write off rules abw","max benefits met adjust","category ii codes","g2211 adjust","96127","99000 denied","misc no show","deductible bill patient","can we adjust this"],
  line:"Read the EOB, classify the balance, then walk one decision path: patient responsibility by EOB, policy or information problem, adjustable by the client’s list, or parked with the decision needed. The path is the same for every client; the answers at each step come from the client’s card.",
  body:[
   "**Step 1: classify from the EOB.** Payment, patient responsibility (deductible, copay, coinsurance), denial, or contractual adjustment. Nothing else happens until you know which.",
   "**Step 2: patient responsibility by the EOB.** Deductible, copay and coinsurance are billable to the patient for every client. Atwood’s protocol says it plainly: processed towards the deductible, bill the patient.",
   "**Step 3: policy or information problem.** Policy inactive on the DOS, COB or questionnaire needed, accident details, wrong demographics: check the client’s column. Restora bills but calls the patient first; One Oak holds and contacts the practice; San Marcos bills because it is the quickest way to get a response.",
   "**Step 4: is it on the client’s adjustment list?** Everyone shares the questionnaire items: TFL exceeded with appeal upheld, small balance under the threshold, exact duplicates, primary paid more than the secondary allowed. Then each client adds its own list.",
   "**Atwood adds:** a CPT denied by primary Medicare and also by the secondary; category II codes denied with the appeal upheld; a CPT denied as maximum benefits met when the other service was already paid.",
   "**Restora adds:** a specific code denied as non-covered under the plan while all other codes paid; athena changed a CPT but the payer paid only the original, adjust the remainder; smoking cessation and CGM analysis are covered once a month, so a max-benefits denial is adjustable; H. pylori 86677 denied; G2211 denied as non-covered once all other codes paid. MISCNOSHOW on a claim is billable to the patient.",
   "**San Marcos adds (dated updates):** coding denials when the appeal or corrected-claim window has passed; the $0.01 sibling claim for risk assessment if denied with the EOB in athena; G0444 billed again within 12 months of a paid one on Medicare and Medicare HMO; G2211 denied as non-covered; 96127 denied (96127 is never billed to insurance: if it holds a claim, remove 96127 and send the rest); any DOS over 365 days found to be outside the filing limit. Refunds are not ours there; Privia handles them, log in CAR.",
   "**Space Coast adds:** TFL with appeal upheld is adjustable, but discuss with management when a TIN update caused it; non-covered depends on the charge. And one warning: two-level procedures paid for one level with the second adjusted off, with no global period on the EOB, is an error to report to the practice immediately, not an adjustment to accept.",
   "**Upstate adds:** 99406 may be adjusted when denied for anything other than a registration problem; patient balances under $24.99 get one attempt then adjustment.",
   "**Step 5: nothing fits.** Park it: CAR or clarification log, with the exact decision needed, the EOB finding and what you already did. Never move a balance to the patient to make a queue shorter."
  ],
  flow:{steps:["Classify the EOB line: payment, patient share, denial or contractual adjustment","Patient share by EOB (deductible, copay, coinsurance) → bill the patient, any client","Policy inactive or information missing → the client’s column decides: bill, call first, or hold","On the shared adjustment list (TFL upheld, small balance, exact duplicate, primary paid more than secondary allowed)? → adjust and cite the rule","On the client’s own list (Atwood, Restora, San Marcos, Space Coast, Upstate items above)? → adjust and cite the rule","Otherwise → CAR or clarification log with the exact decision needed"],to:"The note names the rule or the decision requested"},
  terms:[["Category II codes","Performance-tracking CPT codes (such as 3008F); several payers pay $0 and some clients allow adjustment after an upheld appeal."],["G2211","The visit-complexity add-on; denied as non-covered at some clients and then adjusted."],["Max benefits met","Denial for a service billed more often than the plan allows; adjustable where the client says the frequency rule is correct."],["MISCNOSHOW","Restora’s no-show charge; billable to the patient."],["Sibling claim","A linked claim created with a token amount, for example $0.01 for a risk assessment."],["TIN update","A change of tax identification number; filing problems it causes go to management at Space Coast."]],
  tips:["When you adjust, write the rule that allowed it in the note. ‘Adjusted per client protocol: TFL exceeded, appeal upheld 09/12’ survives an audit; ‘adjusted’ does not.","A balance that is neither patient share nor on a list is a question, not a write-off."],
  remember:"Classify, bill what the EOB makes the patient’s, follow the column for policy and information problems, adjust only from a list and cite it, park the rest with the decision needed.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, protocol sections and dated updates to 29 September 2026 (internal)."}
]},
{id:"abw-d",short:"Client cards",title:"Client cards: one page per practice",blurb:"Where each practice is, what we do for it, and the rules that are specific to it, with the date of the latest update.",lessons:[
 {id:"abw-atwood",title:"Atwood Family Medical Center",
  keywords:["atwood","afmc","atwood family medical","chesapeake","atwood rules","sliding fee"],
  line:"A Privia family-medicine practice in Chesapeake, Virginia. ABW works Hold, insurance AR over 60 days, Zero Pay and coding denials. The practice refuses virtual credit cards.",
  body:[
   "**Scope.** Hold buckets, insurance AR follow-up for claims over 60 days from the date of service, Zero Pay clean-up, and coding-denial work by ABW coders on held claims. Weekly client call.",
   "**Supervising provider and self-pay.** Two physicians each supervise two advanced practice providers (the COM lists the pairs); use the supervising provider as the referring provider when one is needed. Self-pay patients who pay at the visit get a 50% Sliding Fee Schedule discount; patients listed only as self-pay owe the full balance.",
   "**Coding corrections.** Modifiers per athena suggestions, verify with the practice if athena offers none; new-to-established corrections and the basic modifiers with verification if needed; QW on labs yes. Rule 7000, Clinical Order Review, is not overridden: wait and record it in the CAR.",
   "**Bill the patient.** Inactive policy: yes for all carriers; for Medicaid remove the insurance, change the patient to self-pay, then bill. Information needed from the patient: yes. Copay, coinsurance, deductible: yes, and a balance processed to the deductible is billed. Invalid demographics: yes. Non-covered services under the patient’s plan and out-of-network providers: adjust, with no further client approval (ABW, October 2026).",
   "**Adjust.** TFL exceeded with the appeal upheld: yes. Small balances: no. Exact duplicates: yes, with no further approval (ABW, October 2026). Primary paid more than the secondary allowed: yes. Non-covered codes under the provider fee schedule: CAR. Plus the practice’s own list: a CPT denied by primary Medicare and by the secondary; category II codes denied with the appeal upheld; a CPT denied as maximum benefits met when the other service was paid.",
   "**Payments.** Virtual credit cards are not accepted; call the payer for another payment method.",
   "**Credentialing.** PAR or non-PAR status learned on a payer call may be updated in athena; document what the payer said (ABW, October 2026)."
  ],
  terms:[["Advanced practice provider (APP)","A nurse practitioner or physician assistant whose claims carry a supervising physician."],["Supervising provider","The physician athena needs on an APP’s claim; at Atwood also used as the referring provider."]],
  tips:["Medicaid patient with an inactive policy: three steps, not one. Remove the insurance, self-pay, then bill."],
  remember:"No VCC. No small-balance write-offs. Rule 7000 waits in the CAR. Self-pay at the visit: 50% sliding fee.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, Atwood Family Medical Center section, version 1, May 2026 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-oneoak",title:"One Oak Medical Group",
  keywords:["one oak","oneoak","aldie","one oak rules","health assessment","99000","99211 lab draw","z00.00 primary","wellness visit codes","ippe","awv","g0438","g0439","one oak coding"],
  line:"A multispecialty practice in Aldie, Virginia, where ABW works Hold and does front-end coding. Its rules are the most protective of the patient: hold rather than bill when information is missing, and never let a modifier touch a health assessment.",
  body:[
   "**Scope.** Hold buckets and front-end coding of CPT and diagnosis codes from provider notes. Weekly client call.",
   "**Coding corrections.** Modifiers per athena suggestions and the basic modifiers: yes, but never on health assessments. The practice wants every health assessment submitted as a health assessment only, even when extra work was done, unless the note itself says otherwise. New to established: yes. Override rule 7000: yes. QW on labs: yes.",
   "**Bill the patient.** Inactive policy: yes for commercial and Medicare, no for Medicaid. Information needed from the patient, or invalid demographics: do not bill; hold the claim and contact the practice’s named contacts first. Copay, coinsurance, deductible: yes. Non-covered and out-of-network: case by case with the practice.",
   "**Adjust.** TFL with appeal upheld: not without the practice’s review. Small balance: $25. Exact duplicates: yes. Primary paid more than secondary allowed: yes. Non-covered under the contract: hold and ask. Referring provider from the chart and PAR/non-PAR updates: yes. Virtual credit card: call for a check reissue or set up EFT.",
   "**Lab visits (updates of April 2026).** CPT 99000, specimen handling, is billed on all in-house lab draws except physicals; a denied 99000 is adjusted, never billed to the patient. A nurse-scheduled blood draw that is not a physical bills 99211, 36415 and 99000 together, as long as the note carries a brief clinical line such as ‘patient presents for lab draw of X and reports no specific concerns’. A urine collection bills 99211 and 99000. The one-line note is what unlocks 99211.",
   "**Annual exams.** Z00.00 is the primary diagnosis on annual exams. For Medicare wellness the reference the practice shared applies: the Welcome to Medicare visit (IPPE) is G0402 with G0403–G0405 for the screening ECG; the first annual wellness visit is G0438 and later ones G0439; preventive medicine exams for other payers use 99385–99387 for new and 99395–99397 for established patients by age band; social-needs screening G0136 and advance care planning 99497/99498 can accompany them. Quality-measure G-codes (depression screening, tobacco, alcohol, blood pressure, A1c) are reported as the documentation supports."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Situation","One Oak rule"],rows:[["Health assessment with extra work","Bill as health assessment only, no added modifiers unless the note says so"],["Information needed from patient","Hold and contact the practice; do not bill"],["Nurse lab draw, not a physical","99211 + 36415 + 99000 with a one-line clinical note"],["Urine collection","99211 + 99000"],["99000 denied","Adjust; never bill the patient"],["Annual exam","Z00.00 primary"],["Lab retest","Bill as a lab visit"]]},
  terms:[["Health assessment","The practice’s term for its preventive visit; billed as such regardless of extra work."],["99000","Specimen handling and transport; billed on in-house draws except physicals."],["99211","Level-1 nurse visit; needs a documented clinical interaction."],["36415","Routine venipuncture."],["IPPE","Initial Preventive Physical Examination, the Welcome to Medicare visit, G0402."],["AWV","Annual Wellness Visit, G0438 first and G0439 subsequent."]],
  tips:["If the note says ‘Health assessment only’, that is the whole instruction. Modifiers off.","Before billing 99211 on a lab draw, find the one-liner in the note. No line, no 99211."],
  remember:"Hold rather than bill when something is missing. Health assessments stay plain. Lab draw = 99211 + 36415 + 99000 with a note; urine = 99211 + 99000; 99000 denied → adjust.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, One Oak section, version 1 March 2026 with updates to 24 April 2026 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-restora",title:"Restora Medical",
  keywords:["restora","restora medical","orlando","restora rules","misclabfee","miscsupp","pellet","dexcom","holter","nexplanon","s0612","s0610","level 4 and 5","clarification log restora","restora coding","bcbs fl app","1125f","1126f","misc no show"],
  line:"A Privia internal-medicine practice in Orlando, Florida, with the widest ABW scope: Hold and specific Manager Hold items, Zero Pay, insurance and patient AR, front-end coding and coding denials. Its COM carries the longest list of coding and adjustment rules of the six.",
  body:[
   "**Scope.** Hold buckets, Manager Hold for specific claim-change events (not credentialing, web access, overpayment or VCC), Zero Pay, patient AR, insurance AR over 60 days, front-end coding and coding-denial work. Weekly client call.",
   "**Bill the patient.** Yes for inactive policy, missing COB, questionnaire or accident details, and wrong demographics, but call the patient first to obtain the missing details. Copay, coinsurance and deductible: yes. A MISCNOSHOW charge is billable to the patient.",
   "**Adjust.** TFL exceeded with appeal upheld; balances under $5; exact duplicates; primary paid more than the secondary allowed; a specific code denied as non-covered under the plan when all other codes paid; athena changed a CPT but the payer paid only the original, adjust the rest; smoking cessation and CGM analysis denied as max benefits (covered once a month); H. pylori 86677 denied; G2211 denied as non-covered once the other codes paid. Depression screening non-covered may be adjusted; other fee-schedule questions go to the CAR, as do referring-provider additions and PAR/non-PAR updates.",
   "**Credentialing.** Two physicians are not credentialed with one payer each; for those payers the claim is billed with the practice owner as supervising and rendering provider, and the same substitution applies for any other payer where the rendering provider is not enrolled. The COM names them. UMR follows UnitedHealthcare rules; Meritain follows Aetna rules.",
   "**Coding rules.** Any lab performed in-house is codable; outside labs are not. 96127 with an E/M needs a distinct diagnosis, or modifier 59 if none is available. A Z code may be added to a wellness or preventive visit with an E/M after reviewing the record. Comprehensive well-woman exams are S0612 for established and S0610 for new patients; CHAMPVA rejects S0612, so code 99396 for that payer. The practice expects level 4 and 5 visits and wants any level 3 explained in the clarification log, which goes out daily with the claims downloaded and the rationale. EKGs are coded on every chart whatever the result. Hospital discharges are coded by time. A wellness and a problem visit on the same day are both billed; if the payer denies and shifts the full amount to the patient, recode as an office visit only and send a corrected claim. 36415 and 36416 are always coded, even though payers bundle them. When the assessment and plan support Z00.00, bill wellness only. Never bill 1125F or 1126F, the pain-severity codes, to BCBS. A preventive visit correctly denied as max benefits becomes an office visit on a corrected claim. When clonidine is administered during the visit the E/M is billed at level 5 if the documentation supports it. BCBS Florida scrubs APP claims whose supervising and rendering provider differ; make them the same.",
   "**Cash items.** The practice collects $30 for a lab draw, recorded as MISCLABFEE; apply the $30 to that claim and close it. Pellet therapy is self-pay, $400 for women and $650 for men, recorded as MISCSUPP; apply the collected amount and close the claim, and send any other services performed to insurance.",
   "**Common in-house procedures.** Dexcom continuous glucose monitoring: 95249 start-up and training, 95251 monthly interpretation. Holter monitor: 93242 hook-up, 93244 reading. Nexplanon J7307 insertion and 11982 removal. Also pap smears, ear lavage, nebuliser treatments, EKG, pellet therapy, cyst removal, spirometry, pelvic and breast exams, staple and suture removal, abscess drainage and injections.",
   "**Patient AR.** Payment plans are not ours to set: direct patients to the Privia patient line. There are no discounts; say so clearly. If a patient will pay at the office, leave a sticky note on the patient record. Questions and reports go to the office manager and office assistant."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Rule","Detail"],rows:[["Small balance","Under $5 adjust"],["Once-a-month services","Smoking cessation, CGM analysis: max-benefits denial → adjust"],["86677 denied","Adjust"],["G2211 non-covered","Adjust after the other codes pay"],["Well-woman exam","S0612 established, S0610 new; CHAMPVA → 99396"],["BCBS","Never 1125F / 1126F; BCBS-FL APP claims need supervising = rendering"],["Lab draw fee","$30 MISCLABFEE applied and closed"],["Pellet therapy","$400 women / $650 men MISCSUPP applied and closed; other services to insurance"],["Payment plans","Privia patient line; no discounts"]]},
  terms:[["MISCLABFEE","Restora’s $30 lab-draw collection code."],["MISCSUPP","Restora’s self-pay pellet-therapy code."],["MISCNOSHOW","Restora’s no-show charge, billable to the patient."],["S0612 / S0610","Well-woman annual exam codes, established and new."],["Level 4 and 5","The E/M levels the practice expects; level 3 needs a clarification-log explanation."],["Clarification log","Daily list of claims not billed as expected, with rationale."]],
  tips:["Before adjusting anything at Restora, find the line in its list. The list is long because the practice decided each case; a case not on it is a CAR item.","Wellness plus problem visit denied with the patient made responsible: recode as an office visit, corrected claim. Do not bill the patient."],
  remember:"Call before billing the patient. Adjust only from the list. S0612/S0610, never 1125F/1126F to BCBS, cash items applied and closed, plans through Privia.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, Restora Medical section, version 1 May 2026 with addenda to September 2026 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-sanmarcos",title:"San Marcos Family Medicine",
  keywords:["san marcos","smfm","san marcos rules","no medicaid","ndc","j3420","j3301","j7613","j1885","86318","86677 clia","g0444","96127 san marcos","gp modifier","refunds privia","sibling claim"],
  line:"A family-medicine practice in Texas where ABW works Hold and Zero Pay. It takes no Medicaid, bills patients readily, adjusts coding denials once the appeal window is gone, and keeps adding dated rules about specific codes.",
  body:[
   "**Scope.** Hold and Manager Hold buckets and the Zero Pay tracker.",
   "**Coding corrections.** All five yes: modifiers per athena suggestions, new to established, the basic modifiers, override rule 7000, QW on labs.",
   "**Bill the patient.** Inactive policy: yes, and the practice does not accept Medicaid. Information needed from the patient: yes, because the practice finds a bill the quickest way to get a response. Copay, coinsurance, deductible: yes. Invalid demographics: yes. Non-covered and out-of-network: case by case with the practice.",
   "**Adjust.** TFL exceeded with appeal upheld: yes. Small balance: under $5 (ABW, October 2026). Exact duplicates: yes. Primary paid more than secondary allowed: yes. Non-covered under the contract: do not adjust, but track which services are not covered. Referring provider from the chart: verify with the practice. PAR/non-PAR from a call: yes. Virtual credit card: EFT.",
   "**Dated updates.** 31 March: coding denials may be adjusted when the appeal or corrected-claim window has passed; refunds are Privia’s, not ours, so log them in the CAR; the $0.01 sibling claim for risk assessment is processed by the payer and adjusted if denied with the EOB in athena. 7 April: G0444, annual depression screening, billed again within 12 months of a paid one on Medicare or a Medicare HMO is adjusted; an NDC denial means reviewing the clinicals to find the NDC. 21 April: G2211 denied for an invalid modifier, remove the modifier and send a corrected claim; if G2211 has no modifier, check the E/M for modifier 25 and remove it, then resubmit; 86677 denied for CLIA becomes 86318 on a corrected claim. 12 May: claims rejected internally by a coding scrub are overridden and submitted so the payer receives them before the filing limit. 2 June: any DOS over 365 days is checked against the filing limit and adjusted if outside it. 15 September: G2211 denied as non-covered is adjusted; one provider’s claims must never carry the GP modifier, remove it and resubmit if denied. 29 September: 96127 is not billed to insurance; a denied 96127 is adjusted. ABW confirmed (October 2026): if 96127 holds a claim, remove 96127 and submit the remaining eligible services to insurance.",
   "**NDC reference.** J3420 (B12), J3301 (triamcinolone), J7613 (albuterol) and J1885 (ketorolac) each have an NDC recorded in the COM; use the COM table when the payer asks for the NDC."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Code","Rule"],rows:[["G0444","Adjust if billed again within 12 months of a paid one (Medicare, Medicare HMO)"],["G2211","Invalid modifier → remove and resubmit; E/M modifier 25 → remove; non-covered → adjust"],["86677","CLIA denial → 86318 on a corrected claim"],["96127","Never to insurance; denied → adjust; holds a claim → remove 96127 and submit the rest"],["GP modifier","Never on one named provider’s claims"],["Risk assessment sibling claim","$0.01 claim processed; adjust if denied with EOB"],["DOS over 365 days","Check TFL; adjust if outside"]]},
  terms:[["NDC","National Drug Code; the 11-digit product identifier a payer wants on a drug claim."],["Sibling claim","A linked claim created alongside the visit claim, here a $0.01 risk-assessment line."],["Scrub override","Submitting a claim athena rejected internally so the payer receives it before the filing limit."],["GP modifier","Modifier for services under an outpatient physical therapy plan of care."]],
  tips:["San Marcos changes its rules by date. Read the newest entry first; the September rules replace what April said about G2211.","No Medicaid here. If a Medicaid card appears, it is a practice question, not a billing step."],
  remember:"No Medicaid, bill the patient, track non-covered rather than adjust, refunds belong to Privia, and read the dated code rules in order.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, San Marcos section, versions of March and April 2026 with updates to 29 September 2026 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-scsp",title:"Space Coast Spine and Pain",
  keywords:["space coast","scsp","spine and pain","melbourne","pain management","space coast rules","carefirst n55","co96 n55","rendering kick reason","tricare rfa","lumbar rfa mri","dwc-25","dwc form","two level procedure","nasa location"],
  line:"A pain-management practice in Melbourne, Florida. ABW cleaned up its insurance AR and now works Hold. Its rules are permissive on billing and adjusting, but it has three workflows of its own: a payer-defect directive, a TRICARE documentation trend, and the workers’ comp DWC-25 form.",
  body:[
   "**Scope.** Insurance AR follow-up over 60 days as a clean-up project, then Hold buckets ongoing. Weekly client call.",
   "**Coding corrections.** All five yes.",
   "**Bill the patient.** Yes in every case: inactive policy, information needed, cost sharing, invalid demographics. Non-covered and out-of-network go to the practice case by case.",
   "**Adjust.** TFL exceeded with appeal upheld: yes, but discuss with management when the cause was a TIN update. Small balance: under $40. Exact duplicates: yes. Primary paid more than secondary allowed: yes. Non-covered under the contract: depends on the charge and the adjustment. Referring provider from the chart and PAR/non-PAR updates: yes. Virtual credit card: call for a check reissue or EFT.",
   "**Two-level procedures.** A recurring error: two-level procedures paid for one level with the second adjusted off, with nothing on the EOB about a global period, mostly at the NASA location. That is not an adjustment to accept; report it to the practice immediately.",
   "**CareFirst CO96 / N55 (Privia directive, July 2026).** A CareFirst system defect denied claims with remark N55 and routed them to Hold under the kick reason ‘Rendering’. While CareFirst reprocessed them, Privia’s instruction was to hold off on resubmitting, appealing, correcting, rerouting or adjusting those claims, and athena moved them into Manager Hold in large batches. **That project is complete** (ABW, October 2026): the hold-off no longer applies, and any remaining CareFirst N55 account is reviewed and worked on its current status. The COM’s date 07/14/2025 is a typing error for 2026. The pattern still matters: while a payer fixes a defect, stop touching the claims and document the directive.",
   "**TRICARE lumbar RFA.** TRICARE began requesting recent MRI documentation, not just procedure notes, on lumbar radiofrequency ablation claims. Identify the trend on your claims and coordinate with the team before appealing one by one.",
   "**Workers’ comp DWC-25.** Office and clinic claims need the DWC-25 form; hospital claims do not. The full workflow is its own topic under ABW workflows."
  ],
  terms:[["N55","Remark code on the CareFirst defect denials of 2026. The reprocessing project is complete: work any remaining account on its current status."],["MGRHOLD","The Manager Hold kick reason; athena moved the CareFirst defect claims into Manager Hold during the 2026 project, and care centres could route them with it if they preferred."],["RFA","Radiofrequency ablation, a pain procedure TRICARE scrutinises with MRI documentation."],["DWC-25","The Florida workers’ compensation treatment form required on office claims."],["Two-level procedure","A spine procedure billed at two levels; an EOB paying one with the other adjusted off is an error to report."]],
  tips:["A CareFirst N55 left over from the 2026 defect: the reprocessing project is complete, so check the claim’s current status and work it from there.","A TIN-related timely filing denial is a management conversation, not a quiet write-off."],
  remember:"Bill freely, adjust under $40, report two-level errors at once, work leftover CareFirst N55s on their current status, and know when a DWC-25 is required.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, Space Coast section, version 1 May 2026 with updates to 18 August 2026 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-upstate",title:"Upstate Lung and Critical Care Specialists",
  keywords:["upstate","upstate lung","spartanburg","pulmonology","upstate rules","patient ar upstate","collections upstate","99406","bronchoscopy charge entry","vob checklist","sleep study","do not copy the doctor"],
  line:"A lung and critical-care practice in Spartanburg, South Carolina, where ABW’s work is mostly patient AR and Zero Pay, plus inpatient charge entry for the bronchoscopy department, demographics, authorisation and benefits verification. One instruction sits on the contact sheet in bold: do not copy the physician on emails.",
  body:[
   "**Scope.** Zero Pay, patient AR, Hold and Manager Hold, Unpostables, charge entry for consults, bronchoscopies and inpatient visits for three named providers, demographics from the practice’s billing sheet (no eligibility check), prior authorisation and benefits verification for listed appointment types, front-end coding and coding-denial work. The practice manager is the point of contact; a separate contact handles patient escalations.",
   "**Patient AR rules.** Priority buckets 90 and 120 days. Patients on a payment plan are contacted only if they missed a payment. New payment plans may be offered, possibly reducing the monthly amount. Discounts: balances over 24 months old may be discounted 50% or 70%. Up to three call attempts with a one-day gap, during business hours, on the designated line. No patients are off-limits and no consent form is required. Statements go by email on request. A patient who wants to pay by phone is transferred to the practice’s payment line. The practice uses an outside collections agency; accounts are forwarded through athena. Small balance threshold $25. Medicaid patients owe only the $3.30 copay.",
   "**Dated patient AR updates.** 25 March: balances under $24.99 get one attempt, then the balance may be adjusted; balances of $25 or more get up to three attempts, then the claim may be forwarded to collections. 30 April: accounts with a date of service before 2022, no claim since 2021 and no patient payment since 2021 go straight to collections. 28 May: balances with a date of service before 2023, no monthly payments and no patient contact since 2023 are adjusted off.",
   "**Benefits verification checklist.** Effective and termination dates; plan type; provider and facility network status; copay, coinsurance and deductible, including available and met amounts; out-of-pocket maximum, available and met; if the provider is out of network, whether out-of-network benefits exist; whether the plan covers a sleep study.",
   "**Zero Pay.** 99406, smoking-cessation counselling, may be adjusted when denied for anything other than a registration problem.",
   "**Inpatient charge entry.** Claims for service dates from the start date onward, appointment type Bronchoscopy, for the three providers the COM names. Queries go to the practice manager, never copied to the physician."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Patient balance","Action"],rows:[["Under $24.99","One attempt, then adjust"],["$25 or more","Up to three attempts one day apart; then it may go to collections through athena"],["Over 24 months old","Discount of 50% or 70% may be offered"],["DOS before 2022, nothing since 2021","Straight to collections"],["DOS before 2023, no payments or contact since 2023","Adjust off"],["Medicaid","$3.30 copay only"]]},
  terms:[["Payment plan","An agreed monthly amount; contact only on a missed payment."],["Designated line","The phone line ABW uses for patient calls."],["Sleep study coverage","A benefit to confirm during verification for this practice."],["Unpostables","athena’s unmatched payments and EOBs, worked by ABW here."]],
  tips:["Three attempts, one day apart, then collections: the sequence is the rule. A note that shows two attempts is not ready for collections.","Verification here has seven checks, not three. Sleep study coverage is the one people forget."],
  remember:"Patient AR by threshold and age, three attempts one day apart, discounts only over 24 months, never copy the doctor, and a seven-point benefits check.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, Upstate Lung section, versions to June 2026 with updates to 28 May 2026 (internal)."}
]},
{id:"abw-e",short:"ABW workflows",title:"Workflows that trip people up",blurb:"The workers’ comp DWC-25 form, patient AR calls the ABW way, and how to keep up with a manual that changes every week.",lessons:[
 {id:"abw-dwc",title:"Workers’ comp: the DWC-25 workflow",
  keywords:["dwc","dwc-25","dwc form","workers comp form","adjuster","hospital claim dwc","office claim dwc","workers compensation denial abw","dwc not required"],
  line:"A workers’ compensation claim at the pain practice denies for a missing DWC-25 form only when it is an office or clinic claim. Hospital claims do not need the form. Find it, upload it, send it to the adjuster, note it; if it is missing, request it and track the request.",
  body:[
   "**Step 1: identify the claim type.** A hospital claim does not need a DWC-25: make sure the clinical notes are available, document that the form is not required for hospital claims, update the account and resolve the denial. An office or clinic claim needs the form: go to step 2.",
   "**Step 2: look for the form.** Search the patient’s clinical documentation, administrative documentation and the attached documents or forms section. If the DWC-25 is there: download it, attach it to the claim, send it to the adjuster, note the actions, resolve and close the denial.",
   "**Step 3: obtain a missing form.** Ask the treating physician, the clinical staff or the medical records team. Track the request until the form arrives and document every follow-up attempt in the account notes. Once received: upload it, send it to the adjuster, resolve the denial.",
   "**Step 4: the note.** One of five lines, as applicable: DWC-25 located and uploaded; DWC-25 requested from the provider or clinical team; hospital claim, DWC not required; documentation sent to the adjuster; denial resolved and claim updated.",
   "**Step 5: exceptions.** Mark the form ‘Not required’ when the claim is a hospital claim, when the practice’s named contacts confirm it is not needed, or when the documentation requirement was satisfied by clinical notes alone. The account note must show which exception applied."
  ],
  flow:{steps:["Hospital claim? Document ‘DWC not required’, confirm clinical notes, resolve","Office or clinic claim: search clinical docs, admin docs, attached forms","Found: download, attach, send to adjuster, note, resolve","Not found: request from treating physician, clinical staff or medical records; track; note each attempt","Received: upload, send to adjuster, resolve","Exceptions: hospital claim, practice confirms not required, clinical notes sufficient; note which"],to:"Denial resolved with the account note showing the path taken"},
  terms:[["DWC-25","Florida workers’ compensation treatment form the adjuster needs on office and clinic claims."],["Adjuster","The workers’ compensation carrier’s claim handler who receives the form."],["Attached documents","The athena section where scanned forms live; the third place to look."]],
  tips:["Decide hospital or office before you search. Half the DWC denials on hospital claims resolve with one sentence in the note.","A request you did not track is a request that did not happen. Date each follow-up."],
  remember:"Hospital: not required, say so. Office: find it, upload it, send it; or request it, track it, note it.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, Space Coast section, DWC Form Denial Workflow, 18 August 2026 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-patient-ar",title:"Patient AR calls the ABW way",
  keywords:["patient ar calls","calling patients","payment plan","patient discount","three attempts","voicemail patient","collections handoff","patient statement request","pay over the phone","patient balance legitimacy"],
  line:"Patient AR is ABW acting as the practice’s own office: check the balance is real, call to collect or set a plan, request missing information, leave a voicemail when nobody answers, and hand off to collections only by the client’s rule. Discounts and payment plans are the client’s decision, never yours.",
  body:[
   "**Before the call.** Confirm the balance is legitimate from the EOB: deductible, copay, coinsurance, or a client-approved charge such as a no-show fee. An illegitimate balance is adjusted per practice protocol, not collected. Check the client’s special instructions: number of statements sent, last payment date, last statement date, minimum balance, consent requirements.",
   "**The attempts.** The ABW standard is three call attempts with a one-day gap, during the practice’s business hours, on the designated line. When unreachable, leave a voicemail requesting a callback, only if the client permits voicemails, and document each attempt with date and time. After the third attempt the account goes to the client through the CAR; there is no set turnaround time (ABW, October 2026).",
   "**On the call.** Verify the patient. Explain the balance in EOB terms. Offer the payment options the client allows. Payment plans: Restora sends patients to the Privia patient line; Upstate lets ABW set new plans and possibly lower a monthly amount, and contacts patients already on a plan only when they miss a payment. Discounts: Restora has none, say so clearly; Upstate allows 50% or 70% on balances over 24 months old. Pay by phone: Upstate transfers the call to the practice’s payment line; Restora notes a patient who will pay at the office with a sticky note.",
   "**Statements and information.** Send a statement by email on request where the client allows. Use the call to collect missing information too: COB answers, questionnaire responses, accident details, corrected demographics.",
   "**Handoff and write-off.** Upstate: under $24.99 one attempt, then the balance may be adjusted; $25 or more up to three attempts, then it may go to collections through athena; the practice’s outside agency takes it from there. Old balances have their own rules by date of service and last contact. Restora: small balance under $5. Never move an account to collections short of the client’s attempt count.",
   "**What ABW never does.** Hard collections, threats, discounts the client did not approve, or calls outside the client’s hours. Language assistance and patient escalations go to the practice contact the COM names.",
   "**The athena steps.** Verification (name and date of birth), statements, taking payments, cards on file, payment plans and the patient AR note are in ‘Patient calls and payments in athena’."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Question","Restora","Upstate"],rows:[["Payment plans","Privia patient line","ABW may set plans; contact existing plans only on a missed payment"],["Discounts","None","50% or 70% over 24 months old"],["Pay by phone","Sticky note if paying at the office","Transfer to the practice payment line"],["Small balance","Under $5 adjust","Under $24.99 one attempt then adjust"],["Collections","Not in scope","After three attempts; via athena to the outside agency"],["Consent form","Questionnaire pending","Not required"]]},
  terms:[["Legitimate balance","A patient balance the EOB or a client-approved charge supports."],["Designated line","The phone line ABW uses for patient calls; some clients add it to statements."],["Three attempts rule","Three calls, one day apart, before any next step."],["Collection eligibility","The client’s attempt count, statement count and age rules an account must meet."]],
  tips:["Say the balance the way the EOB says it: ‘your plan applied $120 to your deductible’, never ‘the insurance did not pay’.","If the patient asks for a discount at Restora, the answer is a clear no. If they ask at Upstate, check the age of the balance first."],
  remember:"Real balance, three attempts one day apart, plans and discounts only by the client’s rule, collections only after the count, and the practice’s voice throughout.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, Patient AR questionnaires and protocols for Restora and Upstate (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-keep-current",title:"Keeping up with a manual that changes weekly",
  keywords:["keep current","latest update","rule changed","version history","which rule applies","dated update","privia directive","payer defect","mass denial","how to propose a rule","weekly call update"],
  line:"Every ABW rule has a date. New rules arrive on the weekly client call or by email and are added to the COM with that date. The COM itself says its client rules and dates have not been reconciled, so read all of a practice’s entries and confirm the current guidance before acting. Payer-wide directives from Privia override the claim-by-claim routine while they last.",
  body:[
   "**Read every dated entry.** Each client section ends with ‘Update received as of’ entries. Some later entries change earlier ones (San Marcos: April says how to fix a G2211 modifier denial; September says non-covered G2211 is adjusted). Some overlap (Upstate: 30 April sends very old balances to collections; 28 May adjusts very old balances off). The consolidated COM says its rules and dates have not been reconciled: refer to the practice’s section and confirm the current guidance, with the account manager, before you act.",
   "**Version history.** Every COM opens with a version table: who updated it, when, what changed, who approved it and how. ‘Passive approval’ in the approval column means the client did not object. Check that the version you are reading is the current one.",
   "**Payer-wide directives.** When a payer admits a system defect, Privia or ABW may send a directive like the CareFirst N55 one: hold off on resubmitting, appealing, correcting or adjusting the affected claims; let athena move them to Manager Hold (route them yourself only if the practice prefers, with a note); keep front-end work normal; monitor the payer’s stated reprocessing date. Note the directive on every affected account. When the project ends, the account manager updates the instructions: the CareFirst N55 project of July 2026 is complete, and its remaining accounts are worked on their current status.",
   "**Trends.** A payer changing what it asks for, such as TRICARE wanting MRI documentation on lumbar RFA claims, is raised as a trend to the team and the practice, not fought one claim at a time.",
   "**Proposing a change.** If a rule is missing or wrong, put the case in the clarification log with the decision you need, raise it on the weekly call, and wait for the COM to be updated. Until then the existing rule stands.",
   "**If this playbook and the COM disagree,** the COM wins. Send the difference through Feedback so the playbook can be corrected."
  ],
  terms:[["Update received as of","The dated protocol entries at the end of each client section; newest last."],["Directive","A written instruction from Privia or ABW that overrides normal claim handling for named claims."],["Trend","A pattern across claims that is escalated as one issue."]],
  tips:["Note the COM version and the date of the latest update you relied on when you adjust or bill under a client rule.","A rule you remember from last month may have changed. Check before you cite it."],
  remember:"Read every dated entry and confirm the current guidance with the account manager, directives override the routine, trends are raised as one issue, and the COM beats this playbook.",
  source:"ABW Consolidated Client Operations Manual, version histories, dated updates and Privia communications (internal)."}
]},
{id:"abw-f",short:"ABW athena",title:"athenaOne: claims, holds and kick codes",blurb:"Finding your way in athenaOne, the five Hold scenarios, the kick codes, and the work reports.",lessons:[
 {id:"abw-athena-nav",title:"Finding your way in athenaOne",
  keywords:["athenaone","athena one","athena navigation","find patient athena","search by claim id","resources menu","view eobs","view phone payer information","phone payer information","view claim forms","preview primary form","go to claim edits","claim edits","billing summary athena","quickview"],
  line:"Log in with the client’s credentials, find the patient by claim ID or patient ID, and work from the Resources menu: View EOBs, View Phone Payer Information, Preview Primary Form and, above all, Go to Claim Edits, where the claim history lives and where you add the note, status and kick reason.",
  flow:{steps:["Log in to athenaOne with the **credentials the client provided**","Home page → **Find patient** → search by **claim ID** or **patient ID**, whichever the task tells you to use","The patient home page opens: Hold and MGR Hold work happens from here","**Resources** (right-hand side) → choose from the drop-down"],to:"Go to Claim Edits: claim history, notes, status and kick reason"},
  compare:{title:"The Resources menu",head:["Option","What it shows"],rows:[["View EOBs","The EOBs posted to the claim, to analyse a denial"],["View Phone Payer Information","The payer’s name and phone number, the insurance card image, the patient and claim details (claim ID, DOS, total charges) and the provider details (practice, tax ID, NPI, practice and pay-to addresses)"],["Preview Primary Form","The CMS-1500 that was billed"],["Go to Claim Edits","The whole claim history: charges and DX, payments and adjustments with the EOB link, earlier claim history and notes. It is also where you add a note, a status, a kick reason and a post date, and close the account with the right action"]]},
  body:[
   "**Other screens the SOPs use.** **Billing → Billing summary** lists the patient’s other visits, to see whether the same DX and CPT were paid before. **Clinicals → Charts → Find** searches the chart for authorization and medical-record documents. **Quickview** and **Patient Account View** hold the patient-level details: cards on file, payment plans and patient AR notes.",
   "**Minimum necessary.** Phone payer information shows tax IDs, NPIs and patient details. Use them for the call or the appeal; never copy them into training notes, chats or emails that do not need them."
  ],
  terms:[["Go to Claim Edits","athena’s claim page: history, charges, payments, notes, and the controls for status and kick reason."],["Resources menu","The drop-down on the patient page: View EOBs, View Phone Payer Information, View Claim Forms, Preview Primary Form, Go to Claim Edits."],["Billing summary","The list of the patient’s visits and claims, used to compare with earlier paid claims."]],
  tips:["Start every held claim in Go to Claim Edits: the denial, the EOB link and the last notes are on one page.","Search by the identifier the task gives you: claim ID or patient ID."],
  remember:"Log in → Find patient by claim or patient ID → Resources: EOBs, phone payer, primary form, Claim Edits. Notes, status and kick reason go in Claim Edits.",
  source:"ABW Workflow SOP (athenaOne navigation), draft February 2026 (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-hold",title:"Hold and MGR Hold: the five scenarios",
  keywords:["abw hold","athena hold status","mgr hold","mgrhold","manager hold","hold and mgrhold","hold scenarios","authorization hold","auth missing hold","referral hold","abw provider not participating","provider not par hold","diagnosis code invalid","dx invalid athena","cpt invalid","invalid cpt athena","adjust the balance athena","custom transaction code","clinicals charts find","provider grid","abw coding team","abw send to coding"],
  line:"HOLD means athena’s rules found a problem the biller can fix; MGRHOLD means a manager must review and decide. Five scenarios cover most held claims: authorization, referral, provider not participating, invalid diagnosis and invalid CPT. Each starts in Go to Claim Edits.",
  body:[
   "**HOLD.** athena’s rules engine found a coding error, missing information or a payer-rule problem. Review it, correct it and move the claim on, usually to DROP.",
   "**MGRHOLD.** A claim standard staff cannot resolve: complex billing, high-value claims, credentialing problems, a missing modifier or repeated errors that need a wider fix. A manager reviews it, notes the reason, takes the action, and moves it back into the workflow.",
   "**Adjusting a balance.** Go to visit & Claim update → **Actions** → **Adjust the balance** → choose the CPT → enter the kick reason **OTHERADJUST** and the **custom transaction code** → write the claim note → **Adjust Balance**. ABW (October 2026): every adjustment Purview makes uses the kick code OTHERADJUST; record the transaction and status codes that fit the kind of adjustment, and document it in the note. Adjust only where the client’s rules allow it.",
   "**Ask your supervisor.** For invalid CPT or diagnosis claims and provider-not-participating claims, the SOP leaves some steps open. Reach out to your supervisor for: how to send a claim to the coding team; what to do if coding makes no change; how to send an appeal or attach medical records; how to check participation dates without access to the provider grid; how to send a query to the account manager; and where to get the provider’s details when the payer asks for an appeal."
  ],
  compare:{title:"The five Hold scenarios",head:["Scenario","Check","Then"],rows:[
   ["Authorization","Is the auth number in the client system?","Not on the claim: add it and bill with status DROP and kick reason DRPBILLING. On the claim: check it was entered correctly and covers the DOS, the units, the patient and the provider; if you change anything, use DRPBILLING. No auth and the payer needs records: Clinicals → Charts → Find the authorization documents for the DOS, note it, status PLSAPPEAL (athena asks for an appeal note for the payer)"],
   ["Referral","Is the referral number in the client system?","As for authorization: add or correct it with DROP and DRPBILLING. No referral and records needed: find the approvals for the DOS, note it, status PLSAPPEAL"],
   ["Provider not participating","The rejection reason","Call the payer for the provider’s participating dates. Participating on the DOS: ask for reprocessing. Not participating: record the effective dates and follow the COM"],
   ["Diagnosis code invalid","Which CPT the DX is on","Billing → Billing summary: were the same DX and CPT paid on the patient’s other visits? Yes: call the payer to reprocess. No: send the claim to the coding team"],
   ["CPT invalid","Which CPT is invalid","The same check: paid before with the same DX and CPT → call to reprocess; never paid → the coding team"]]},
  terms:[["HOLD","A claim stopped by athena’s rules for a fixable problem: coding, missing information or payer rules."],["MGRHOLD","A claim that needs a manager’s review and decision before it can go on."],["PLSAPPEAL","The code that has athena appeal the claim with the supporting documents."],["Custom transaction code","The client-specific code recorded with an adjustment."]],
  tips:["Changed something on the claim? Drop it with DRPBILLING. The payer needs records? PLSAPPEAL.","Adjusting a balance? The kick code is always OTHERADJUST; record the transaction and status codes that fit the adjustment, and document it.","Invalid CPT or diagnosis, or provider not participating, and the SOP doesn’t say how to do a step? Reach out to your supervisor."],
  remember:"Auth or referral: add or fix → DROP + DRPBILLING; records needed → PLSAPPEAL. Not participating: call for the dates. Invalid DX or CPT: paid before → reprocess; never paid → coding team. Adjusting → OTHERADJUST. Unsure how to do a step? Ask your supervisor.",
  source:"ABW Workflow SOP, Hold and MGR Hold scenarios; ABW Consolidated SOP, Hold and MGRHOLD workflow (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-kick-codes",title:"Kick codes: which one, and when",
  keywords:["kick code","kick codes","which kick code","kick code list","otheradjust","adjustment kick code","saag","saag guidelines","kick file","kick files","drpbilling","drppatient","inform kick code","webaccessgranted","ptresp","plsappeal","adnreview","tertiary kick code","rvclose","cip kick code","cbohold","fwrpayornonresponsive","fwrconfirmdeposit","fwrsharedtinremit","abandoned kick code"],
  line:"A kick code records why you acted and decides where the claim goes next: back to the payer (DRPBILLING), to the patient (DRPPATIENT, PTRESP), to athena’s team (PLSAPPEAL, ADNREVIEW, TERTIARY), to the practice (MGRHOLD), closed (RVCLOSE), adjusted (OTHERADJUST), or nowhere at all (INFORM, a note only).",
  compare:[
   {title:"Kick codes you use",head:["When","Kick code","What happens"],rows:[
    ["Billing information (CPT, DX, POS) corrected; ready to resubmit","DRPBILLING","The claim is resubmitted to the payer"],
    ["A genuine balance the patient owes needs billing","DRPPATIENT","The patient is billed"],
    ["Insurance processed the claim and set the patient’s responsibility","PTRESP","The balance moves to the patient"],
    ["A note only: a call or an update, without moving the claim; also when placing an account in the CAR for the account manager to escalate","INFORM","Status unchanged; the work is recorded"],
    ["Payer portal credentials added so athena can download and post EOBs","WEBACCESSGRANTED","athena resumes its follow-up"],
    ["Denied for medical necessity; athena to appeal with the documents","PLSAPPEAL","To CBOHOLD, athena’s team"],
    ["Denied for unspecified or complex additional information","ADNREVIEW","To CBOHOLD; athena calls the payer"],
    ["A tertiary claim must go out (rare)","TERTIARY","To CBOHOLD; athena submits it"],
    ["The practice manager must review (rare; only when requested)","MGRHOLD","To MGRHOLD, always with a note saying what the manager must do"],
    ["You adjust a balance (any adjustment Purview makes)","OTHERADJUST","The adjustment is recorded; add the transaction and status codes that fit it, and document it"],
    ["Balance is $0, or the claim needs no more follow-up","RVCLOSE","Closed; the balance must already be $0"]]},
   {title:"Codes athena applies: know what they mean",head:["Kick code","Meaning"],rows:[
    ["FWRPAYORNONRESPONSIVE","athena could not reach the payer after several attempts; the practice side steps in"],
    ["FWRCONFIRMDEPOSIT","A remittance arrived with no matching deposit in athena; check whether the payment went to a non-athena bank account"],
    ["FWRSHAREDTINREMIT","The payment went to another entity that shares the tax ID"],
    ["ABANDONED","A credit balance closed without a refund, or a small balance written off after final efforts"]]}
  ],
  body:[
   "**SAAG.** ABW also calls its kick-code guidelines SAAG, or the kick file: the same codes and the situations they are for.",
   "**Ask your supervisor.** For the SAAG file itself, or when a kick code here does not seem to fit the situation on your claim, ask your supervisor before you act.",
   "**Avoid CIP for notes.** CIP (claim in progress) puts the claim in athena’s queue, where it can go unaddressed, and ages it. To leave a note, use INFORM.",
   "**The FWR codes** appear when athena has finished its standard follow-up and the responsibility shifts back to the practice side. Read them: they tell you what athena already tried.",
   "**Client rules first.** A kick code carries out a decision; it does not make it. Bill or move a balance to the patient, adjust or close only where the EOB and the client’s rules in the COM allow it."
  ],
  terms:[["Kick code (kick reason)","The code chosen with a claim action that records why and sends the claim to its next queue."],["SAAG (kick file)","ABW’s name for its kick-code guidelines: which kick code to use in which situation."],["CBOHOLD","athena’s own work queue: PLSAPPEAL, ADNREVIEW and TERTIARY send claims there."],["CIP","Claim in progress; avoid it for notes, because it ages the claim."]],
  tips:["Changed anything on the claim? DRPBILLING. Only writing a note? INFORM.","MGRHOLD without a note is an unfinished action: the manager must know what to decide.","Not sure a kick code fits, or need the SAAG file? Ask your supervisor."],
  remember:"Fixed → DRPBILLING. Patient owes → DRPPATIENT or PTRESP. Note only → INFORM, never CIP. athena appeals → PLSAPPEAL; athena asks the payer → ADNREVIEW. Manager → MGRHOLD with a note. Adjusting → OTHERADJUST. $0 → RVCLOSE.",
  source:"ABW Workflow SOP, scenario-based kick code usage (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-reports",title:"Running the three work reports",
  keywords:["how to run reports","run report athena","activity wizard","hold report","mgrhold report","zero pay report","zero pay review report","unpostables report","find unpostables","download csv","comma delimited"],
  line:"Three reports feed the work: Hold and MGRHOLD from the Activity Wizard, Zero Pay from Zero Pay Review (three months at most), and Unpostables from Find Unpostables.",
  compare:{title:"Where each report comes from",head:["Report","Path","Settings"],rows:[
   ["Hold and MGRHOLD","Reports → Activity Wizard → Advanced","Date range (for example the previous month); service department type, supervising provider, rendering provider, insurance reporting category and transfer type set to Show All; tick Show patient insurance ID, Show Claim ID and Show date of service; claim status HOLD and MGRHOLD; format comma-delimited text → Run Report"],
   ["Zero Pay","Claims → Zero Pay Review → New report","Date range of 3 months at most; add the service department; add the insurance reporting category if needed → Run report → tick the CPT codes the client wants → open the total charges → Export"],
   ["Unpostables","Financials → Unpostables → Find Unpostables","Status Open; tick the category (Remittance Required, Unmatched athenaNet charge, Patient, Forward balance) → Generate worklist → Download CSV"]]},
  body:["**All three end in a file.** Hold and MGRHOLD as comma-delimited text, Zero Pay through Export, and Unpostables as a CSV."],
  tips:["Tick Show Claim ID and Show date of service on the Hold report: the claim ID is how you open each claim.","Zero Pay: keep the date range to three months or less."],
  remember:"Hold: Activity Wizard (Advanced), HOLD + MGRHOLD, comma-delimited. Zero Pay: New report, 3 months at most, Export. Unpostables: Find Unpostables, Open, category → CSV.",
  source:"ABW How to run Reports; ABW Consolidated SOP (internal)."}
]},
{id:"abw-g",short:"ABW payments",title:"Zero Pay, Unpostables and patient payments",blurb:"Reviewing zero-paid claims, clearing the money athena could not place, and taking patient payments in athena.",lessons:[
 {id:"abw-zero-pay",title:"Zero Pay review",
  keywords:["zero pay","zero pay review","zero payments","legacy zero pay review","zero pay workflow","adjusted by athena","zero dollar claim","zero paid claim","payment mismatch tracker","cci edits zero pay","high dollar zero pay","zero pay tracker"],
  line:"ABW works the Zero Pay tracker: claims closed at $0 after athena adjusted them, for primary payers only. Check each closure or adjustment; refile or appeal what can still be collected; ask the account manager when unsure.",
  flow:{steps:["**Claims → Zero Pay Review** → **Legacy Zero Pay Review**","Sort by trend; filter **Adjustments made by: athena** → **Run Report**","Open the count: the claims list on the left → open each claim → **Resources → Go to Claim Edits**","All three claim statuses show **closed**: check the payments **CPT by CPT**","Read the **EOB**: why was it adjusted?","Choose the closing option that matches your review → **Submit**","Unsure → the account manager"]},
  body:[
   "**Patterns first.** Export the list and mark what needs research. Trends by payer, CPT or provider point to a systemic problem: a payer’s reimbursement policy or a coding issue. Work the **high-dollar** zero pays first; that is where most of the money is.",
   "**For each claim.** Read the ERA or EOB reason codes: athena shows a contractual adjustment as a $0 payment for review. Check CCI edits: a $0 payment can mean a National Correct Coding Initiative edit was broken, so verify the codes before appealing. Where allowables are set up, the **Payment Mismatch Tracker** under Claims shows expected against received. Edit the claim from the billing summary when it needs a correction, such as an updated diagnosis.",
   "**Then.** Appeal a zero pay that was the payer’s error, tell the team about recurring problems with examples, and document every resolution.",
   "**Every dollar amount.** Work denied claims of every amount in Zero Pay; there is no $200 cut-off (ABW, October 2026)."
  ],
  terms:[["Zero Pay","Claims processed and closed at $0, reviewed to confirm each closure or adjustment was right."],["Payment Mismatch Tracker","The Claims report that compares expected payments with what was received."],["CCI edit","A National Correct Coding Initiative pairing rule; breaking one can produce a $0 payment."]],
  tips:["A $0 line can be a correct contractual adjustment. Read the EOB before you call it an error.","Primary payers only: a zero pay from a secondary payer is not this workflow."],
  remember:"Primary payers, claims athena adjusted to $0, every dollar amount. Patterns first, high dollars first, the EOB for each, refile or appeal what is collectable, document; unsure → the account manager.",
  source:"ABW Workflow SOP, Zero Payments; ABW Consolidated SOP, Zero Pay Review workflow; COM scope of work (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-unpostables",title:"Unpostables: remittance, deposits and balances athena could not place",
  keywords:["unpostables","unpostable","remittance required","review missing remittance","missing remittance","confirm deposit","upload eob athena","work records","vcc expired","virtual credit card athena","w9 request","w-9 request","check reissue","unmatched athena charge","unmatched athenanet charge","forward balance","balance fwd","fcn icn","withholding wo","fully worked receivables"],
  line:"An unpostable is money athena could not apply on its own: the remittance is missing, the deposit is unconfirmed, the charge does not match, or a forward balance needs placing. ABW reviews and resolves what athena puts on the dashboard and escalates the rest.",
  body:[
   "**Find them.** Financials → Unpostables → Find Unpostables → status **Open** → tick the category → Generate worklist (Download CSV for Excel).",
   "**Remittance Required (Review Missing Remittance).** athena has the payment but not the remittance. Open the record → **Work records** → upload the EOB (choose the file, preview, upload) → **Actions** → untick the box to close it → note where the remittance came from → **Update Record**. It leaves the Remittance Required column. In this queue you only upload the remittance: no posting.",
   "**Confirm Deposit.** athena has the EOB but the payment details need confirming. View the EOB and check the mode of payment. A $0.00 check with no check number: look on the EOB for a denial or an offset; if there is no offset EOB, call the payer for it, then act on what you find. When the client confirms the deposit, click **Confirm deposit** and fill in the yellow fields. For a check, find out the date it was cashed.",
   "**Virtual credit cards.** athena does not process VCC payments. An expired VCC: call the payer and ask for the payment by check or EFT, within the client’s VCC rule.",
   "**Ask first.** A payer asking for a W-9 → the account manager. Before asking a payer to reissue a check → confirm with the client that you may.",
   "**Unmatched athenaNet charge or patient.** A charge from an outside system that athena could not match to an appointment: the message lacked data (patient, codes, dates), the patient’s Quickview or insurance was not current, or a newer charge message replaced the original for the same patient, procedure and date.",
   "**Forward balances.** After a data migration: patient → Ledger tab → Charges tab → **Balance Fwd**, with the date transferred, the responsible party and notes. On an ERA: the forward balance (FB) carries a financial control number (FCN) that matches the ICN of the adjusted claim. Compare it with the earlier payment: if the earlier payment was higher, the provider owes the difference and the FB is negative; if lower, the FB is positive and the payer owes. An FB not recovered promptly can become a withholding (WO) on a later remittance.",
   "**Corrected claims after an overpayment.** Post the payer’s reversal (often a $0 remit) first, then the corrected payment. If negative charges or adjustments will not post, a support case may be needed to enable them."
  ],
  compare:{title:"The categories",head:["Category","What is missing","Your move"],rows:[["Remittance Required","The remittance (athena has the payment)","Upload the EOB, note its source, close; no posting"],["Confirm Deposit","Confirmation of the payment (athena has the EOB)","Check the EOB and payment mode; $0 check → denial or offset; client confirms → Confirm deposit"],["Unmatched athenaNet charge / patient","A match to an appointment or patient","Find the missing data; Quickview and insurance must be current"],["Forward balance","Where an old or recouped balance belongs","Balance Fwd on the ledger, or track the ERA’s FB against the ICN"]]},
  terms:[["Unpostable","A payment or remittance athena could not apply automatically."],["Forward balance (FB)","An amount carried to a later remittance; negative when the provider owes."],["FCN and ICN","The financial control number on the ERA and the internal control number of the adjusted claim; they match."],["WO (withholding)","An unrecovered forward balance taken from a later payment."],["Fully Worked Receivables","athena’s worklist for items that could not be posted or collected, marked fully worked."]],
  tips:["Remittance Required is an upload task. If you find yourself posting, you are in the wrong queue.","A $0 check is a clue, not an answer. Find the denial or the offset before you close it."],
  remember:"Find → Open → category. Remittance Required: upload, note, close. Confirm Deposit: EOB, payment mode; $0 → offset or denial; client confirms → confirm. VCC: not in athena; expired → check or EFT. W-9 and reissues: ask first.",
  source:"ABW Unpostables Workflow; ABW Workflow SOP, Unpostables; COM scope of work (internal)."},
 {id:"abw-patient-pay",title:"Patient calls and payments in athena",
  keywords:["abw patient verification","verify patient abw","patient call verification","two step verification","verify patient name dob","yellow alert","inbound patient call","outbound patient call","patient call etiquette","tsicollect","collections balance athena","set next statement date","collect patient payments","credit card plus","card on file","save a new card","payment plan athena","add payment plan","prepayment plan","patient account view","document patient ar","initials abw","superbill","show patient activity","view billing summary"],
  line:"Verify the patient (name and date of birth) before anything else, check the balance is real and not already in collections, never disclose the contractual adjustment, stop after three calls, and take payments, cards and plans only through athena’s screens, with the card number never written anywhere.",
  body:[
   "**Before the call.** Read the patient notes and the yellow alert, and check when the last call or letter went out. Check whether any amount has gone to collections: that amount is not ours, and a balance under Collect (TSICOLLECT) is already being processed. **View billing summary** shows the outstanding balance; **Show Patient Activity** shows when the patient last paid. Make sure the balance in the patient bucket is a true patient balance, and check the statements sent.",
   "**On the call.** Say you are calling from the billing company. Verify two things: the patient’s **name** and **date of birth**; nothing goes further until the patient is verified. If someone else answers, ask whether the patient agrees to share the information and whether they look after the bills; a spouse or parent who handles the account may continue. If a caller is abusive, give the disclaimer that the call cannot continue in that tone; do not just hang up. Do not give the patient a timeframe to call back. Explain the payment plans and what happens if the bill is not paid. A patient eligible for a discount the client allows gets it when paying on the same call.",
   "**What never to say.** The contractual adjustment is never disclosed to the patient.",
   "**COB denials.** Ask the patient to update the coordination of benefits with the payer, then call back with the same update, so the claim can be resubmitted or reprocessed.",
   "**Three calls.** Make and document three call attempts; there is no set turnaround time. After the third attempt the account goes to the client through the CAR for review and guidance, and the next follow-up depends on the outcome and the client’s instructions (ABW, October 2026). Where a client’s COM sets its own rule, such as One Oak’s, follow it.",
   "**Statements.** Billing → **Set next statement date**: choose tomorrow’s date, never today’s.",
   "**Taking a payment.** Billing → **Collect Patient Payments**. The Credit Card Plus pop-up must appear; without it you cannot take the payment, so ask the account manager. Enter the amount next to the outstanding amount, not below it. Most payments are for today’s or a future service; ‘payment for an old service’ is only for a discount on an older service. Fill in the yellow fields: card number, expiry date, the name as on the card, and tick Use patient address. An Amex card needs the 4-digit security code. **Never save a card number anywhere.** Patients can also pay through the athenaOne portal (Communicator → Log in to portal as patient shows what they see).",
   "**Payment for an old service (ABW, October 2026).** Review the original balance, the discount and anything the patient already paid. Apply the discount only as the approved policy allows, and make sure the payment shows against the outstanding balance. If the payment is more than the new patient balance, do not refund or adjust the excess yourself: get approval and follow the refund or payment-adjustment process. If the discount or the payment handling is unclear, hold the account and ask before you change anything.",
   "**Card on file.** View patient appointments → the oldest DOS on which the claim was created (not a rescheduled or cancelled one) → Patient tab → **Save a new card**. The card authorization ends either at an amount or at a date; the most a card on file can hold is **$1,500**. athena attaches the card-on-file agreement itself. Quickview → Details shows a card on file and any email sent about it. If an account with a card on file reaches you, check the card’s expiry and remaining limit; if it is valid with room left, nothing more is needed.",
   "**Payment plans and prepayment plans.** Only where the client lets ABW set them (Restora sends patients to the Privia line; Upstate lets ABW set plans). Quickview → **Add Payment Plan**: name, type, payment method (charge the card if an active card is on file, otherwise send statements), frequency and date agreed with the patient, start date, any down payment, the claims covered → Save plan and enter the card details. A plan can be stopped at the patient’s or provider’s request. **Add PrePayment plan** (under insurance): name, type, service department, provider (the supervising provider if unsure), service date, code and description only if sure, balance, due date, instalments, shown on the patient view, monthly reminders on.",
   "**Documentation.** Quickview → **Patient Account View** → the action (how you communicated) → the note. No card details in any note. End the note with your initials and **ABW**. Patient AR is documented only in Patient Account View; **patient contact** is documented in two places: on the claim, and in the patient account notes with the claim number.",
   "**Other requests.** Payments made by cash or check: View patient activity → post date Show all → Show patient payment summary. A superbill must be requested from the practice; ABW has no access to it."
  ],
  compare:{title:"Quick answers",head:["Situation","Do this"],rows:[["The caller is not the patient","Ask whether the patient agrees to share and whether they handle the bills"],["Balance under TSICOLLECT, or sent to collections","Leave it"],["Three calls made, no result","The account manager, unless the COM sets the next step"],["Next statement date","Tomorrow, never today"],["No Credit Card Plus pop-up","You cannot take the payment: the account manager"],["Card on file limit","$1,500"],["Superbill requested","The practice provides it"]]},
  terms:[["Yellow alert","The patient-record alert to read before any call."],["TSICOLLECT","The collections status; balances under it are already being processed."],["Credit Card Plus","athena’s card-payment service; its pop-up must appear before you can collect."],["Patient Account View","Where patient AR notes are written, ending with your initials and ABW."]],
  tips:["Name and date of birth first, on every call, even when the patient called you.","Write what was agreed in the note, never the card number."],
  remember:"Verify name and DOB. Real balance, not in collections. Never the contractual adjustment. Three calls, then the account manager. Statement date: tomorrow. Card numbers never saved; card on file up to $1,500. Notes in Patient Account View, ending with initials and ABW.",
  source:"ABW Workflow SOP, Patient AR; ABW Consolidated SOP, Patient AR (internal)."}
]}
]}
];

/* ── Assessments: short, per module; the final draws across the playbook ── */
const SOP_QUIZ=[
{t:"ABW basics",q:"A Client Operations Manual counts as approved when:",correct:"The client sends no feedback after it is emailed (passive approval)",wrongs:["The account manager signs it","Privia countersigns it","The client reads it aloud on the weekly call"],ex:"The Atwood and Restora COM disclaimers: once emailed, the COM is an automatic sign-off if no additional input or feedback is received; Upstate’s version history records ‘Passive approval’."},
{t:"ABW basics",q:"A held claim cannot be resolved by ABW effort. Where does it go?",correct:"The clarification log or CAR, with the exact decision needed",wrongs:["Adjusted off, so that the Hold queue stays clear","To a patient statement, so the patient can call in","Left in Hold until the practice’s next audit"],ex:"Unresolvable claims are escalated for practice assistance, and the entry must state the decision needed, not just ‘pending client’."},
{t:"ABW basics",q:"How does the manual describe ABW’s patient AR work?",correct:"An extension of the practice team; no hard collections, never a collections agency",wrongs:["A pre-collections service that sends accounts to agencies","A legal recovery team that issues demand letters","A collections agency that works on commission"],ex:"ABW calls, sets plans and requests information as the practice’s own office would. Collections handoff happens only by the client’s rule."},
{t:"ABW basics",q:"athena shows no clear status and no EOB. Before you write ‘no status available’ you:",correct:"Check the payer portal and ECHO where applicable",wrongs:["Write it; athena is the system of record","Check only the clearinghouse","Ask the patient"],ex:"The workflow reminder: athena, then the payer portal, then ECHO, before concluding nothing is available."},
{t:"ABW basics",q:"Where Insurance AR follow-up is in scope (Atwood, Restora, Space Coast), it covers claims older than:",correct:"60 days from the date of service, the client’s preference",wrongs:["30 days from the claim submission date","120 days from the date of service","Any claim that still has a balance"],ex:"The Atwood, Restora and Space Coast scopes: ‘over 60 days from DOS (Client Preference)’. At Space Coast it was a clean-up project, with issues worked per the practice protocols."},
{t:"ABW basics",q:"The consolidated COM says its client rules and dates have not been reconciled. Before acting on a client rule, you:",correct:"Read the practice’s section and confirm the current guidance",wrongs:["Follow the oldest entry, since it was approved first","Follow whichever entry is quickest to apply","Apply the matching rule from another practice"],ex:"The consolidated COM: ‘Client-specific rules and dates have not been reconciled or independently updated; refer to the relevant practice section and confirm current guidance before operational use.’"},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"Which of these is NOT one of the six parts of a standard ABW note?",correct:"The biller’s opinion of whether the denial is fair",wrongs:["The source checked and the date","The verified status in the payer’s words","The next step with owner and follow-up date"],ex:"Notes carry facts confirmed by the payer, portal, EOB/ERA or client, and the action done. Opinions are not documentation."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"A claim is still in process at the payer. The note describes it as:",correct:"Received / in process / pending, with a recheck date per the payer’s timeframe",wrongs:["Approved, since no denial has been received from the payer","Paid, pending the EFT, with no recheck needed","Closed until the payer sends a final EOB"],ex:"Never describe a pending claim as approved. Record the status as the portal or rep stated it and when to recheck."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"Beyond the standard note parts, a call note must add:",correct:"Date and time, the rep’s name or ID if given, and the reference number",wrongs:["The rep’s mood, the hold time and the queue length","A summary of the payer’s website and phone menu","The patient’s diagnosis and full date of birth"],ex:"Without the reference number the call cannot be proven later. Date, time, rep and reference every time."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"The ABW posting note ‘Payment received; EOB uploaded. Batch #____. Payment needs to be posted.’ may be used only when:",correct:"Payment and EOB are actually in hand, with the batch number",wrongs:["The portal shows the claim as approved for payment","The patient says they have already paid the balance","A payment is expected from the payer next week"],ex:"The AR Notes guide: use the posting note only when payment and EOB are actually available, and replace every placeholder (such as the batch number) before saving."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"A clearinghouse rejected the claim. The note must say:",correct:"Payer acceptance is not confirmed, what was corrected, the resubmission date and trace number",wrongs:["The payer denied the claim and an appeal is due","The claim is in process at the payer, with a recheck date","The patient must be billed while the claim is reworked"],ex:"The clearinghouse-rejection template: payer acceptance is not confirmed; record the correction, the resubmission date and trace number, and confirm acceptance."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"An EOB shows an out-of-network or non-covered reason. Billing the patient waits until:",correct:"Contract and benefit terms are validated and the client gives direction",wrongs:["The next statement cycle runs for the patient","The payer confirms the denial again by phone","The balance is over $100 and older than 60 days"],ex:"The AR Notes template: validate contract and benefit terms and obtain client direction; do not bill the patient until validated. In the questionnaires One Oak, San Marcos and Space Coast want these cases brought to them, Restora answered ‘Bill patient’, and Atwood adjusts them without further approval (ABW, October 2026)."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"‘Appeal sent 10/14 via portal with records, confirmation #4471, follow up 10/28.’ Which required fact is missing?",correct:"The verified appeal deadline and the denial reason",wrongs:["The representative’s name and ID","The patient’s date of birth and address","The claim’s billed amount and the CPT codes"],ex:"Appeals record six facts: denial reason, verified deadline, method and date, documents, confirmation number, next follow-up."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"A handoff note for a claim waiting on the practice must:",correct:"State the exact decision the practice needs to make",wrongs:["Say ‘pending client’","Copy the denial wording only","List every call made that month"],ex:"The workflow reminder: for pending client assistance state the exact decision needed, never only ‘pending client’."},
{t:"ABW notes",q:"When may a note say ‘voicemail left’?",correct:"Only when one was actually left and the client permits voicemails",wrongs:["Whenever the line rang out with no answer","Whenever the patient has a mobile number on file","Always, to show that a call attempt was made"],ex:"Document only actions actually completed. A failed call is noted as unsuccessful with a retry date."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"The five areas of the Hold / Manager Hold questionnaire are:",correct:"Denial coding corrections, bill the patient, adjustments, provider credentialing, and deposit confirmation (VCC)",wrongs:["Scheduling, coding, billing, payment posting and patient collections","Eligibility, authorization, coding, claim submission and appeals","Front end, mid cycle, back end, reporting, analytics and compliance"],ex:"Eighteen questions in five areas: denial coding corrections (5), bill patient (5), adjustment (5), provider credentialing and enrolment (2), and deposit confirmation cases (1, about virtual credit cards)."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"A client left a questionnaire answer blank. You:",correct:"Park the claim in the CAR or clarification log and ask; a blank is not a yes",wrongs:["Treat the blank as a yes and act on the claim","Follow Atwood’s answer to the same question","Adjust the balance, since nothing forbids it"],ex:"The AR Notes guide: adjust denials only when supported by the EOB, client instructions and the required approval; for client input, state the decision needed, and the account stays pending until direction is received."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"At Atwood the policy was inactive on the DOS and the patient has Medicaid. You:",correct:"Remove the insurance, change the patient to self-pay, then bill the patient",wrongs:["Bill Medicaid anyway, as the policy was billed first","Adjust the balance as an inactive-policy write-off","Hold the claim and contact the practice first"],ex:"Atwood’s answer: yes to all carriers, but with Medicaid remove the insurance and switch to self-pay before billing."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"At One Oak the policy was inactive on the DOS and the patient has Medicaid. You:",correct:"Do not bill the patient; One Oak allows it for commercial and Medicare only",wrongs:["Bill the patient, as for any inactive policy","Bill Medicaid as the secondary payer instead","Adjust it under One Oak’s $25 small-balance rule"],ex:"One Oak’s answer: yes for commercial and Medicare, no for Medicaid."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"At One Oak the payer needs COB information from the patient. You:",correct:"Hold the claim and contact the practice first; do not bill the patient",wrongs:["Bill the patient, since a bill gets the quickest response","Adjust the claim and note the missing COB details","Resubmit with the COB marked as no other coverage"],ex:"One Oak holds and asks; San Marcos, by contrast, bills because it gets the quickest response."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"Small-balance thresholds: Restora, One Oak and Space Coast are, in order:",correct:"$5, $25, $40",wrongs:["$25, $25, $25","$5, $40, $25","$10, $20, $30"],ex:"Restora under $5, One Oak $25, Space Coast $40. Atwood allows no small-balance adjustments; San Marcos is under $5 (ABW, October 2026)."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"San Marcos’s small-balance adjustment limit is:",correct:"Under $5",wrongs:["Under $25","Under $40","There is no limit set"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): San Marcos may adjust small balances under $5."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"Timely filing exceeded, no proof, appeal upheld, at One Oak. You:",correct:"Do not adjust without the practice’s review",wrongs:["Adjust; it is on the shared list","Bill the patient","Resubmit as a corrected claim"],ex:"Most clients allow this adjustment; One Oak wants it reviewed with its named contacts first."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"A claim at Atwood needs a referring provider. You use:",correct:"The supervising provider",wrongs:["The PCP from Quick View","The rendering provider","Any provider in the group"],ex:"Atwood’s answer to the referring-provider question: take the supervising provider as referring."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"A payer wants to pay Atwood by virtual credit card. You:",correct:"Decline; Atwood does not accept VCC. Call for another payment method",wrongs:["Accept it within athena’s time limit for the card","Hold the payment in Unpostables until it expires","Ask the patient to pay the balance instead"],ex:"Atwood: ‘We do not accept VCC, call for other payment.’ One Oak and Space Coast call for a check or EFT; San Marcos wants EFT."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"athena rule 7000, Clinical Order Review, is holding an Atwood claim. You:",correct:"Wait and record it in the CAR; Atwood does not allow the override",wrongs:["Override it, as you would at One Oak","Delete the clinical order and drop the claim","Bill the patient while the order is reviewed"],ex:"One Oak, San Marcos and Space Coast allow the override; Atwood answered ‘we will wait and update in CAR report’."},
{t:"Client rules",q:"A balance is not patient responsibility by the EOB, is on none of the client’s adjustment lists, and no correction, resubmission or appeal applies. You:",correct:"Park it in the CAR or clarification log with the exact decision needed",wrongs:["Adjust it off under the small-balance rule","Move the balance to the patient’s responsibility","Resubmit the claim to the payer once more"],ex:"The AR Notes guide: do not move balances to patient responsibility without validation; for pending client assistance, state the exact decision needed."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Restora, established patient, comprehensive well-woman exam. The code is:",correct:"S0612 (S0610 for a new patient); 99396 if the payer is CHAMPVA",wrongs:["99396 for every payer, new or established","G0439, the annual wellness visit code","99213 with modifier 25 for every payer"],ex:"Restora codes well-woman exams S0612/S0610; CHAMPVA rejects S0612, so that payer gets 99396."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"At Restora, codes 1125F and 1126F are:",correct:"Never billed to BCBS, which does not cover pain-severity reporting",wrongs:["Required on every BCBS claim for pain visits","Billed to Medicare only, never to commercial","Adjusted off whenever the payer pays them"],ex:"The COM: BCBS doesn’t cover services related to pain severity; do not bill 1125F or 1126F on BCBS claims."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Restora billed a wellness visit and a problem visit on the same day. The payer denied them as not billable together and made the patient responsible. You:",correct:"Recode as an office visit only, remove the wellness, and send a corrected claim",wrongs:["Bill the patient the full amount the payer shifted","Appeal the denial with the provider’s note","Adjust both lines off as not billable together"],ex:"The protocol: if the payer shifts the full responsibility to the patient, recode to office visit only and submit a corrected claim."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Pellet therapy at Restora is handled by:",correct:"Self-pay as MISCSUPP ($400 women, $650 men), applied and closed; other services to insurance",wrongs:["Billing the insurer for the pellets along with the visit codes","Writing the pellet charge off as a non-covered service","Billing the pellets as a 99213 office visit to insurance"],ex:"Pellets are self-pay collected by the practice. The MISCSUPP charge takes the collected amount and the claim closes."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"A Restora patient asks for a payment plan. You:",correct:"The Privia patient line: ABW sets no plans there, and no discounts",wrongs:["Set up three monthly payments for them in athena","Offer 20% off if they pay the balance today","Transfer the call to the office manager to decide"],ex:"Restora’s patient AR answers: plans go through Privia, and tell patients clearly there are no discounts."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"San Marcos: CPT 86677 denied for a CLIA issue. You:",correct:"Change it to 86318 and send a corrected claim",wrongs:["Adjust it","Appeal with the CLIA certificate","Bill the patient"],ex:"The 21 April update: 86677 denied for CLIA becomes 86318 on a corrected claim."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"San Marcos and CPT 96127 (from 29 September 2026):",correct:"Never billed to insurance; a denied 96127 is adjusted",wrongs:["Billed with modifier 59 to insurance on every visit it appears","Billed to the patient whenever insurance denies it","Appealed with the screening results when denied"],ex:"The 29 September update: 96127 is not billed to insurance, and a denied 96127 is adjusted. ABW (October 2026): if it holds a claim, remove it and send the rest."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"A San Marcos claim is held because of CPT 96127. You:",correct:"Remove 96127 and submit the remaining services to insurance",wrongs:["Override the hold and submit the whole claim as it is","Bill 96127 to the patient and keep the claim on hold","Adjust the whole claim off as a non-billable visit"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): CPT 96127 is not billed to insurance; if it holds a claim, remove it and submit the remaining eligible services."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Atwood: an exact duplicate claim. You:",correct:"Adjust it; no further client approval is needed",wrongs:["Park it in the CAR for the practice to decide","Resubmit it to the payer as a corrected claim","Bill the patient for the duplicated charges"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): at Atwood the team may adjust exact duplicates, non-covered services and out-of-network providers without additional client approval."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"San Marcos: G0444 denied because one was paid 8 months ago on a Medicare HMO. You:",correct:"Adjust the denied charge",wrongs:["Appeal: screenings are always covered","Bill the patient","Change it to 96127"],ex:"The 7 April update: G0444 billed again within 12 months of a paid one on Medicare or Medicare HMO is adjusted."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"A refund issue appears on a San Marcos account. You:",correct:"Do not work it; Privia handles refunds. Record the account in the CAR",wrongs:["Issue the refund from athena and note it","Offset the refund against the patient’s next claim","Call the payer to set up a recoupment"],ex:"The 31 March update: refunds are handled directly by Privia; update these accounts in the CAR."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Space Coast: a two-level procedure was paid for one level and the second was adjusted off, with no global period shown on the EOB. You:",correct:"Report it to the practice immediately as an incorrect adjustment",wrongs:["Accept the adjustment","Bill the patient for the second level","Resubmit the second level as a new claim"],ex:"A recurring error the practice wants reported at once, especially at the NASA location."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Space Coast: timely filing denied, no proof, appeal upheld, and the cause was a TIN update. You:",correct:"Discuss with management before adjusting",wrongs:["Adjust; it is on the shared list","Bill the patient","Resubmit under the old TIN"],ex:"Space Coast allows the TFL adjustment, but TIN-related cases go to management first."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Upstate patient AR: a balance of $18 and a balance of $60. The attempt rules are:",correct:"$18: one attempt, then it may be adjusted. $60: up to three attempts a day apart, then it may go to collections",wrongs:["Three attempts a day apart for both balances, then collections","One attempt for both balances, then straight to collections","No calls on either; both balances are adjusted off at once"],ex:"The 25 March update: under $24.99 one attempt, then the remaining balance may be adjusted; $25 or more three contact attempts, then the claim may be forwarded to collections. ABW (October 2026): after three documented attempts, the account goes to the client through the CAR."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"At Upstate, a discount may be offered when:",correct:"The balance is over 24 months old: 50% or 70%",wrongs:["The patient asks nicely","The balance is under $25","The patient pays by card"],ex:"Upstate’s answer to the discount question: over 24 months old, 50% or 70%."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Which item is on Upstate’s benefits-verification checklist and easy to forget?",correct:"Whether the plan covers a sleep study",wrongs:["The patient’s employer","The PCP’s NPI","The pharmacy benefit manager"],ex:"Upstate’s benefits-verification list has seven checks; the last is ‘Does the patient plan cover a sleep study?’"},
{t:"Client cards",q:"One Oak nurse-scheduled blood draw, not a physical, with a one-line clinical note. The codes are:",correct:"99211, 36415 and 99000 together",wrongs:["36415 only","99213 and 36415","99000 only"],ex:"The April 2026 update: 99211 is billable with a brief clinical line, plus 36415 venipuncture and 99000 specimen handling. Urine collection is 99211 and 99000."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"One Oak: CPT 99000 was denied. You:",correct:"Adjust it; never bill the patient for it",wrongs:["Bill the patient","Appeal","Rebill as 36415"],ex:"The specimen-handling update: denied 99000 fees are adjusted, not billed to patients."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"A One Oak health assessment visit involved extra work. It is billed as:",correct:"A health assessment only, with no added modifiers unless the note says so",wrongs:["Health assessment plus a level 4 E/M with modifier 25","An office visit","Whatever athena suggests"],ex:"The practice wants all health assessments submitted as health assessments only; modifiers on them only when its note explicitly says."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"An Atwood self-pay patient paid at the time of service. The practice’s rule is:",correct:"50% off as the Sliding Fee Schedule discount; ‘self-pay’ alone gets none",wrongs:["No discount for any self-pay patient at Atwood","A 25% discount on request, recorded as a courtesy","Bill the full amount now and refund half later"],ex:"Atwood’s self-pay protocol: pay at the visit, 50% off via the Sliding Fee Schedule; listed as self-pay only, full balance."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"Atwood: a CPT was denied by primary Medicare and then by the secondary. You:",correct:"Adjust it",wrongs:["Bill the patient","Appeal both","Send to the CAR"],ex:"Atwood’s Hold protocol: a code denied by primary Medicare and also by the secondary can be adjusted."},
{t:"Client cards",q:"At Upstate, emails about claims must never:",correct:"Copy the physician",wrongs:["Mention the claim number","Go to the practice manager","Be sent on a Friday"],ex:"The contact sheet note: please do not copy the doctor on emails. The practice manager is the contact."},
{t:"ABW workflows",q:"At Space Coast, a workers’ comp hospital claim is denied for a missing DWC-25. You:",correct:"Note that hospital claims need no DWC-25, confirm the clinical notes, resolve",wrongs:["Request the form from the treating physician and wait","Bill the patient for the workers’ comp claim","Appeal with the MRI and the procedure note"],ex:"Space Coast’s DWC Form Denial Workflow (update of 18 August 2026), step 1: document that the DWC form is not required for hospital claims."},
{t:"ABW workflows",q:"At Space Coast, an office claim needs a DWC-25 and it is not in the clinical, administrative or attached documents. You:",correct:"Request it from the provider, clinical staff or records; track and note every follow-up",wrongs:["Mark it not required and resolve the denial","Send the adjuster the procedure note instead of the DWC-25","Adjust the claim off as a workers’ comp denial"],ex:"Step 3: obtain the missing form and document each attempt until it arrives, then upload and send it to the adjuster."},
{t:"ABW workflows",q:"At Space Coast, a DWC-25 may be marked ‘Not required’ when:",correct:"Hospital claim, the practice confirms it is not needed, or clinical notes sufficed",wrongs:["The adjuster did not answer the phone after two tries","The claim balance is under Space Coast’s $40 threshold","The patient is on Medicare as well as workers’ comp"],ex:"Step 5 lists the three exceptions, and the note must show which one applied."},
{t:"ABW workflows",q:"The standard ABW patient-call cadence is:",correct:"Three attempts with a one-day gap between them",wrongs:["One attempt per statement cycle","Daily calls until contact","Five attempts in one day"],ex:"Both patient AR questionnaires ask the practice to confirm three attempts with a one-day gap; Upstate said yes."},
{t:"ABW workflows",q:"An Upstate patient is already on a payment plan. You contact them:",correct:"Only if they have missed a payment",wrongs:["Monthly, to confirm the plan","Never","Whenever the balance changes"],ex:"Upstate’s answer: contact patients on a plan only if they have missed a payment."},
{t:"ABW workflows",q:"The CareFirst N55 reprocessing project (July 2026) is complete. A remaining CareFirst N55 account is:",correct:"Reviewed and worked based on its current status",wrongs:["Left untouched, as the hold-off directive still applies","Adjusted off as a payer defect without any review","Moved to Manager Hold with MGRHOLD and left there"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): the project finished on time; the hold-off applied only to that project and is no longer active. Work any remaining account on its current status."},
{t:"ABW workflows",q:"Two Upstate updates seem to cover the same old balances (30 April: collections; 28 May: adjust off). You:",correct:"Confirm the current guidance with the account manager before acting",wrongs:["Apply the 28 May rule, since the newer one always wins","Apply the 30 April rule, since it came first","Pick whichever rule clears the balance fastest"],ex:"The consolidated COM: client rules and dates have not been reconciled; confirm current guidance before operational use. The ABW SOP: if there is any doubt, reach the account manager for clarification."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"In athenaOne, where do you see the EOBs posted to a claim?",correct:"Resources → View EOBs",wrongs:["Billing → Set next statement date","Clinicals → Charts → Find","Financials → Unpostables"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: View EOBs helps you navigate the EOBs posted to the claim to analyse the denial."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Where do you add a note, a status and a kick reason, and close the account?",correct:"Go to Claim Edits",wrongs:["View EOBs","Preview Primary Form","View Phone Payer Information"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: Go to Claim Edits holds the claim history and is where you add notes, status, kick reason and post date, and close the account."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Which Resources option shows the CMS-1500 that was billed?",correct:"Preview Primary Form",wrongs:["View EOBs","View Phone Payer Information","Collect Patient Payments"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: Preview Primary Form shows the CMS-1500 billed."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Hold: the authorization was missing and you have added it. Status and kick reason:",correct:"Status DROP with kick reason DRPBILLING",wrongs:["Status HOLD with kick reason INFORM","Status MGRHOLD with kick reason MGRHOLD","Status CLOSED with kick reason RVCLOSE"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP, Scenario 1: update the auth and bill the claim using status DROP and kick reason DRPBILLING."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"No kick code in the training seems to fit the situation on your claim. You:",correct:"Ask your supervisor before you act",wrongs:["Use the closest code and move on","Leave the claim with no kick code","Use CIP until someone notices it"],ex:"For the SAAG file (the kick-code guidelines), or when a kick code does not seem to fit, ask your supervisor."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"You adjust a balance on an ABW account. Which kick code?",correct:"OTHERADJUST",wrongs:["ABANDONED","RVCLOSE","INFORM"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): use OTHERADJUST for any adjustment Purview makes; record the transaction and status codes that fit the adjustment, and document it."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Hold: no authorization exists and the payer needs medical records. You:",correct:"Find the documents under Clinicals → Charts, note it, set PLSAPPEAL",wrongs:["Bill the patient for the whole visit, as no auth exists","Close the claim with RVCLOSE as an authorization denial","Resubmit the claim unchanged with DRPBILLING and wait"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: no authorization and records needed → Clinicals → Charts → Find the authorization documents for the DOS, document the notes and select status PLSAPPEAL."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Hold: the provider is not participating. Your first step is to:",correct:"Call the payer for the provider’s participating dates",wrongs:["Resubmit the claim unchanged with DRPBILLING","Bill the patient as an out-of-network visit","Adjust the balance off and close the claim"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP, Scenario 3: check the rejection reason and call the insurance for the participating dates; participating on the DOS → reprocess; not → follow the COM."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Hold: invalid DX, and the same DX and CPT were paid on the patient’s other visits. You:",correct:"Call the payer and ask it to reprocess",wrongs:["Send the claim to the coding team","Change the DX yourself and drop it","Adjust the charge off as non-covered"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP, Scenario 4: if the other visits are getting paid, call the insurance and reprocess the claim; if not, send it to the coding team."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Hold: invalid CPT, and the same DX and CPT were never paid on other visits. You:",correct:"Send the claim to the coding team",wrongs:["Call the payer to reprocess it","Close the claim with RVCLOSE","Bill the patient for the CPT"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP, Scenario 5: if we are not getting payments, send the claim to the coding team."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"You corrected the CPT, DX or POS and the claim is ready to resubmit. Kick code:",correct:"DRPBILLING",wrongs:["DRPPATIENT","INFORM","RVCLOSE"],ex:"Kick codes: DRPBILLING is used when billing information (CPT, DX, POS) has been corrected and the claim is ready to be resubmitted."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"You want to record a payer call without moving the claim. Kick code:",correct:"INFORM",wrongs:["CIP","MGRHOLD","ADNREVIEW"],ex:"Kick codes: INFORM leaves notes without moving the claim or changing its workflow status. Do not use CIP for notes."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Why not use CIP just to leave a note?",correct:"It ages the claim; use INFORM instead",wrongs:["It closes the claim straight away","It bills the patient for the balance","It sends the claim to the practice"],ex:"Kick codes: do not use CIP (claim in progress) just to leave a note, as this unnecessarily ages the claim; use INFORM."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Denied for medical necessity; athena should appeal with the documents. Kick code:",correct:"PLSAPPEAL",wrongs:["ADNREVIEW","DRPBILLING","TERTIARY"],ex:"Kick codes: PLSAPPEAL instructs athena to appeal a claim denied for medical necessity with supporting documentation (the claim moves to CBOHOLD)."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Denied for unspecified extra information; athena should call the payer. Kick code:",correct:"ADNREVIEW",wrongs:["PLSAPPEAL","INFORM","WEBACCESSGRANTED"],ex:"Kick codes: ADNREVIEW sends the claim to athena to call the insurance for clarification."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Insurance processed the claim and set the patient’s share. To move the balance to the patient:",correct:"PTRESP",wrongs:["RVCLOSE","DRPBILLING","ADNREVIEW"],ex:"Kick codes: PTRESP transfers the balance to the patient after insurance has processed the claim and determined the patient’s responsibility; only where the EOB and the client’s rules support it."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"A genuine balance the patient owes needs billing. Kick code:",correct:"DRPPATIENT",wrongs:["DRPBILLING","INFORM","TERTIARY"],ex:"Kick codes: DRPPATIENT is used to bill a patient for a genuine balance they owe."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"The claim balance is $0 and it needs no more follow-up. Kick code:",correct:"RVCLOSE",wrongs:["ABANDONED","MGRHOLD","CIP"],ex:"Kick codes: RVCLOSE closes a claim with a zero balance or one that should no longer be followed up; the balance must be $0."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"When you send a claim to MGRHOLD, you must:",correct:"Add a note saying what the manager needs to do",wrongs:["Use it for every denial you work","Leave the note for the manager to write","Also set DRPBILLING on the claim"],ex:"Kick codes: MGRHOLD is rarely used unless requested; always attach a note explaining what the manager needs to do."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"You added payer portal logins so athena can download and post EOBs. Kick code:",correct:"WEBACCESSGRANTED",wrongs:["INFORM","ADNREVIEW","FWRCONFIRMDEPOSIT"],ex:"Kick codes: WEBACCESSGRANTED tells athena that portal credentials for a payer have been added, so it can resume follow-up."},
{t:"ABW athena",q:"Which report lists the HOLD and MGRHOLD claims?",correct:"Activity Wizard (Advanced), claim status HOLD and MGRHOLD",wrongs:["Workflow Dashboard, sorted by department","Zero Pay Review, run as a New report","Find Unpostables, with the status Open"],ex:"How to run Reports: Reports → Activity Wizard → Advanced; claim status HOLD and MGRHOLD; comma-delimited text."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"Zero Pay review covers claims from:",correct:"Primary payers only",wrongs:["Secondary payers only","Patients with a balance","Every payer, tertiary too"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: zero payments are only applicable for primary payers."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"In Legacy Zero Pay Review, ‘Adjustments made by’ is filtered to:",correct:"athena",wrongs:["The practice","The payer","The patient"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: for Adjustments made by, filter by athena, then run the report."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"A Zero Pay report can cover a date range of at most:",correct:"3 months",wrongs:["1 month","6 months","12 months"],ex:"How to run Reports: the date range shouldn’t be longer than 3 months."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"In Zero Pay, which denied claims do you work?",correct:"Every amount: there is no dollar cut-off",wrongs:["Only denied claims of $200.00 or more","Only denied claims under $200.00","Only high-dollar claims over $1,000"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): the $200 figure is removed from the Zero Pay instructions; work denied claims of every amount."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"Which zero-pay claims do you work first?",correct:"The high-dollar ones",wrongs:["The oldest small ones","The ones with patient balances","The ones in alphabetical order"],ex:"ABW Consolidated SOP: focus on high-dollar zero pays first; most of the recovery opportunity is in high-dollar claims."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"Remittance Required: you have uploaded the EOB. Next:",correct:"Note where it came from and close it; no posting",wrongs:["Post the payment to the claim","Send the claim to MGRHOLD","Call the patient about the balance"],ex:"Unpostables: in the Remittance Required queue only upload the remittance, document where it came from and update the record; no further steps such as payment posting."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"Confirm Deposit: a $0.00 check with no check number. You:",correct:"Check the EOB for a denial or offset; no offset EOB → call for it",wrongs:["Confirm the deposit straight away and close the record","Post the $0 payment and close the unpostable record","Ask the payer to reissue the check for the full amount"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: if the check amount is $0.00 with no check number, check the EOB for a denial or an offset; if there is no offset EOB, call and request it."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"A virtual credit card payment has expired. You:",correct:"Call the payer to reissue it by check or EFT",wrongs:["Process the card in athena","Bill the patient for the amount","Write the payment off"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: athena is not processing VCC payments; if the VCC expired, call the payer and request the payment by check or EFT."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"A payer asks for a W-9. You:",correct:"Ask the account manager for the details",wrongs:["Fill it in from the claim form","Ignore it and post the payment","Send the practice’s tax return"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: if the payer is asking for the W-9 form, reach the account manager for details."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"On an ERA, the earlier payment was higher than the corrected one. The forward balance is:",correct:"Negative: the provider owes the difference",wrongs:["Positive: the payer owes the provider","Zero, so nothing needs tracking","The patient’s responsibility"],ex:"Unpostables Workflow: if the previous payment was higher, the provider owes the difference and the forward balance is negative."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"Before discussing a balance, you verify the patient’s:",correct:"Name and date of birth",wrongs:["Address and phone number","Insurance ID and group number","Last payment date and amount"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: two-step verification, patient name and date of birth; you cannot proceed until the patient is verified."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"How many documented call attempts before a patient account goes to the client through the CAR?",correct:"Three",wrongs:["One","Five","As many as it takes"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): make and document three call attempts, then share the account with the client through the CAR; there is no set turnaround time."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"After an approved discount on an old service, the patient has paid more than they now owe. You:",correct:"Get approval and follow the refund or payment-adjustment process",wrongs:["Refund the excess to the patient’s card straight away","Adjust the excess off so that the account shows zero","Keep the excess as a credit toward the patient’s next visit"],ex:"ABW (October 2026): do not refund or adjust the excess yourself; get approval and follow the refund or payment-adjustment process. If anything is unclear, hold the account and ask."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"A balance sits under TSICOLLECT. You:",correct:"Leave it; it is already being processed",wrongs:["Call the patient about it","Adjust it as a small balance","Restart the patient statements"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: a balance under Collect (TSICOLLECT) is being processed; no need to follow up on it."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"Setting the next statement date, you choose:",correct:"Tomorrow’s date, never today’s",wrongs:["Today’s date","30 days from today","The date of service"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: do not select today’s date; update tomorrow’s date."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"What must never be told to the patient?",correct:"The contractual adjustment",wrongs:["Their deductible","Their outstanding balance","The date they last paid"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: never disclose the contractual adjustment to the patient."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"Patient contact is documented:",correct:"On the claim, and in the account notes with the claim number",wrongs:["Only in the Patient Account View, as for patient AR","Only on the claim itself, never in the account notes","Only in an email to the practice after the call"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: patient contact is documented in two places: on the claim itself, and in the patient account notes with the claim number."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"The most a card on file can hold is:",correct:"$1,500",wrongs:["$500","$1,000","$2,500"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: the maximum amount that can be set on a card on file is $1,500.00."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"A patient AR note ends with:",correct:"Your initials and ABW",wrongs:["The payer reference number","The patient’s date of birth","The card’s last four digits"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: document your initials at the end and mention ABW; never put card information in the documentation."},
{t:"ABW payments",q:"A patient asks for a superbill. You:",correct:"Ask them to request it from the practice",wrongs:["Print it from athena for them","Email them the EOB instead","Send them the claim form"],ex:"ABW Workflow SOP: ABW has no access to the superbill; the patient requests it from the practice."}
];
